\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  Central Banks in Asia Boost Efforts to Deal with 'Hot Money' Flows-3-\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alex Frangos   \par \uc2 920 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2013\par 15:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Newswires English Content\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG--Central banks in Asia, Australia and New Zealand are ratcheting up moves to deal with an influx of capital that is keeping \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  strong and complicating efforts to manage growth.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Wednesday it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to blunt the rise of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and would continue to do so, a day after \uc2 Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut interest rates to their lowest level ever and noted the stubborn strength of the Australian dollar. Elsewhere, China is moving to curb bets on the rising yuan while Thailand is mulling effects to curb the strongest level of the baht since the 1997 Asian financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 Driving the surging flows of capital to the region are the ultra-loose monetary policies of the developed world that have forced interest rates to near zero, leading investors to look for higher-yielding assets such as those offered in Asia. Last week's interest-rate cut by the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  added to continuing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japan and the U.S. that are pumping capital into the global financial system. The recent pickup in China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains against the dollar has also attracted speculators inside and outside China to dive into yuan, putting pressure on policy makers there to stem the inflows.\par \par \uc2 "Asia is still where the growth is, relatively speaking. It's quite clear where their [investors'] money will go. Asia is benefiting from that front," said Craig Chan, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst for Nomura in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 Data from provider EPFR Global show that investors have added nearly $8 billion to Asian emerging-market bond mutual funds so far this year, similar in size to the strong growth of capital inflows that occurred in 2010. A \uc2 World Bank\uc2  analysis of flows to emerging markets globally showed an increase through April of 42% over the year earlier, to $64 billion.\par \par \uc2 Another measure: Asia's central banks are again scooping up capital inflows and putting them into foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. \uc2 World Bank\uc2  data show developing Asian economies have added $120 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves so far this year to bring total reserves to nearly $4.3 trillion.\par \par \uc2 While attracting investment from overseas is often a good thing, left unchecked, inflows strengthen local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which causes countries' goods to become less competitive on the global market. In addition, government policy makers worry, money that arrives quickly can leave just as fast, destabilizing local banking, stock and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 "The dilemma these central banks face is they could respond to inflows by cutting interest rates...but in some economies you have overheating risks and asset bubbles" that would be made worse with easier monetary policy, said Stephen Schwartz, economist for \uc2 BBVA\uc2  in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 Philippines, which recently was upgraded to investment-grade status by two ratings companies, has been fighting hot money inflows with several measures, including making it easier for locals to take money abroad and lowering a deposit rate for banks that do cross-border business.\par \par \uc2 The effect in Asia isn't uniform, as the U.S. dollar has had broad strength globally. Some countries, such as India and Indonesia, have seen their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  stay weak in reaction to trade deficits. Compared to 2010, when Asian central banks routinely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, this year has been less dramatic.\par \par \uc2 China moved Sunday to stanch upward pressure on its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by forcing banks and exporters to sell yuan in favor of dollars. Regulators also issued a warning to exporters, which have been suspected of sneaking money into the country through accounting maneuvers related to the invoicing of goods. The moves are meant to dissuade Chinese banks and investors from trying to speculate on the strengthening of the yuan against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been under pressure from the government to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively to tamp down the strength of the baht. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  issued a statement on April 30 noting that the "rapid appreciation" of the baht was "not justified by economic fundamentals." The bank promised to work with the government on measures to respond. No official disclosures have been made, but analysts expect either a rate cut or other moves to stem capital inflows.\par \par \uc2 Nearby, the Philippines has also cut rates on certain deposits last month to slow so-called hot money flows.\par \par \uc2 So far, though, the moves haven't made much of a dent in these strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . While \uc2 Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday cut interest rates for the seventh time since late 2011, the Australian dollar has fallen only about two cents against its U.S. counterpart over that time period.\par \par \uc2 Buoying the Australian dollar has been strong demand from overseas investors. More than 70% of Australian government bonds are held offshore--double the level in foreign hands a decade ago.\par \par \uc2 In nearby New Zealand, the head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the bank has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to slow the appreciation of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  but admitted there was only so much it could do.\par \par \uc2 "We would not expect, given the strength of the flows, that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would materially change the level of the exchange rate, but we could take potentially the tops off rallies," said \uc2 Graeme Wheeler\uc2 , governor of the Reserve Bank of New Zealand.\par \par \uc2 --Rebecca Howard and Rachel Pannett contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Alex Frangos at alex.frangos@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 08-05-13 1323GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020130508e958000kx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
