\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: RBNZ's Wheeler Assures Market It's Business As Usual\b0\par\par\uc2 690 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2012\par 03:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 --RBNZ Governor Graeme Wheeler says quantitative easing unnecessary, bank has scope to cut cash rate\par \par \uc2 --Mr. Wheeler says \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unlikely to have sustained effect\par \par \uc2 --NZ dollar dips on speech, but pares losses as comments digested\par \par \uc2 (Recasts the first paragraph, adds details throughout, comments from economists from paragraph nine.)\par \par \uc2 By Lucy Craymer\par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON--The maiden speech by Reserve Bank of New Zealand Governor Graeme Wheeler assured the market that it would be business as usual at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even as he retained the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or a cut to the official cash rate.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Wheeler told the Admirals' Breakfast Club in Auckland in a speech Friday that price stability and financial stability remained the Reserve Bank's central objectives for monetary and prudential policy, and this had been reinforced by the newly signed Policy Targets Agreement.\par \par \uc2 While annual inflation is currently at 0.8%, below the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 1%-3% target band, Mr. Wheeler said the new focus of keeping inflation at 2.0% should "help to anchor inflation expectations around the mid-point" going forward.\par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar knee-jerked lower following the release of the speech, touching US$0.8175, but pared losses as the market digested Mr. Wheeler's comments and is now at US$0.8193.\par \par \uc2 HiFX\uc2  Senior Trader Stuart Ive said comments that the RBNZ had scope to cut rates had weighed on the New Zealand dollar, but taken alongside comments about price stability they suggest "he will not cut rates on the basis that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is too high" but will only ease if the economy deteriorates.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Wheeler used much of his speech to address recent calls from opposition parties in New Zealand for the RBNZ to either use quantitative easing to reduce the strength of the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar and improve the economy or to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Opposition parties have argued that the high New Zealand dollar has been putting pressure on the export sector.\par \par \uc2 "New Zealand does not require quantitative easing: the economy is growing at an annual rate of around 2%, and the Reserve Bank has scope to lower interest rates if needed," he said.\par \par \uc2 He added that while the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would like to see a lower exchange rate, foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in unlikely to have a sustainable effect on the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar.\par \par \uc2 "The bank will remain vigilant on its criteria for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and will be prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if all its conditions are met," he said.\par \par \uc2 The RBNZ last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in June 2007 and its policy only allows for it to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar is deemed to be exceptionally high or low; when it believes the exchange rate is unjustifiable by economic fundamentals; where \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is consistent with the PTA; and where markets are such that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a reasonable chance of success.\par \par \uc2 "He is not an uber-dove. He is not going to revamp the monetary policy framework or introduce quantitative easing and declare a futile war on the exchange rate," ANZ economist Mark Smith said in a note after the speech.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has held the official cash rate at 2.5% since March 2012 and economists expect it will remain at this level until late 2013. Mr. Wheeler's speech hasn't changed this view, with some economists even thinking the newly arrived governor is less dovish than they had expected.\par \par \uc2 "The over-riding impression we form from this speech is that at this stage it is very much 'business as usual' at the RBNZ under the new governor," \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  economist Darren Gibbs said. "The speech reinforces our sense that at this stage the RBNZ has no more than a soft near-term easing bias and that it will still take significant bad news on activity and/or weak inflation indicators to cause the bank to act on that bias."\par \par \uc2 Write to Lucy Craymer at lucy.craymer@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 25, 2012 21:11 ET (01:11 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020121026e8aq0002e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
