\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:NZ PM Says Kiwi Overvalued, But Doesn't Believe In  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 343 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 April 2012\par 08:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- NZ PM doesn't believe in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 -- NZ PM says high NZD not all bad, helps keep inflation curbed\par \par \uc2 (Adds background about Key's comments in Indonesia in the third paragraph, NZD rate in the seventh and background about upcoming rate decision and economist's comment in the eighth paragraph)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    By Lucy Craymer \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 WELLINGTON (Dow Jones)--New Zealand Prime Minister John Key said Monday he wouldn't support Reserve Bank Of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 New Zealand intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, even though he sees the local dollar as overvalued.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I don't believe in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , I never have and, frankly, I never will," Key said at a press conference.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The New Zealand dollar fell last week after Key, on a trip to Indonesia, called the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  overvalued and said the government was "considering what we can do to resist a rising exchange rate."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Monday, Key said the government would continue to keep its spending under control to reduce the work of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Dreaming that we can somehow get the exchange rate under control through \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is la-la-land stuff," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He added that a high New Zealand dollar wasn't all bad, as it keeps inflation under control and reduces pressure on the RBNZ to act.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The New Zealand dollar, which was trading at US$0.8162 as of 0540 GMT, has gained around 5% since the beginning of the year. Economists expect the RBNZ to continue jawboning the Kiwi lower at the bank's rates meeting Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We expect the fire-and-brimstone warnings about the detrimental effect of the high exchange rate to be repeated, or even intensified," \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \uc2 \f2  Bank Chief Economist Dominick Stephens said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At its March meeting, the Reserve Bank said "sustained strength in the New Zealand dollar would reduce the need for future increases in the OCR."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Rebecca Howard, Dow Jones Newswires; 64-4-471-5990; rebecca.howard@dowjones.com [ 23-04-12 0603GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1138 4190   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120423e84n000at\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
