\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro consolidates with focus on US jobs, EU action\b0\par\par\uc2 757 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Dezember 2011\par 00:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Markets awaiting U.S. non-farm payrolls data\par \par \uc2 * Euro seen consolidating in Asian session\par \par \uc2 * Next week's ECB, EU meets key to year-end sentiment\par \par \uc2 * Kiwi higher, fails to break above this week's high\par \par \uc2 By Antoni Slodkowski\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 2 (Reuters) - The euro struggled to make much headway on Friday, consolidating this week's gains as traders stuck to the sidelines ahead of a crucial U.S. jobs report and event-packed week that could prove decisive for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bloc.\par \par \uc2 The non-farm payrolls report is expected to show an increase of 122,000 jobs and a steady unemployment rate of 9.0 percent. A positive surprise would underpin a recent string of solid U.S. data and bolster risk sentiment, while a weaker-than-expected outcome may push investors to take more profits on recent gains.\par \par \uc2 The euro changed hands at $1.3460 against $1.3457 late in New York. It was off a one-week peak of $1.3534 hit on Wednesday after major central banks moved to ease a credit squeeze stemming from the crisis, sparking a rally.\par \par \uc2 "The move by the central banks simply eased liquidity worries for now. The big-bazooka solutions are coming next week and the euro's strength hinges on their feasibility," said Sumino Kamei, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at the \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 French and German leaders are meeting next Monday to outline joint proposals to put to a Dec. 9 EU summit, seen as -- yet another -- make-or-break meeting for the 12-year-old \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bloc.\par \par \uc2 "EU leaders have disappointed in the past, so nobody is naive enough to simply pile into the euro on mere statements, especially since Europe faces serious economic headwinds anyway," Kamei said.\par \par \uc2 She added, however, that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may move another leg higher if the actions are strongly supported by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and even if the move up is not sustained, it may still prove an important chance to sell into a rally.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hinted on Thursday it was ready to move more aggressively to tackle the crisis if politicians agree on much tighter budget controls in the euro zone, though it stopped short of detailing what exact measures it would take.\par \par \uc2 Still, there is no agreement among EU policymakers regarding how such controls could be implemented and many other problems, including securing resources to leverage the euro zone's bailout fund, linger unresolved.\par \par \uc2 Economists expect the ECB to help banks and an economy on the verge of recession by cutting interest rates next week and announcing longer-term cheap liquidity tenders with easier collateral rules. Markets are pricing in a 25 basis point cut to 1.0 percent at the ECB's Dec. 8. policy meeting.\par \par \uc2 Decent stop-loss euro bids were spotted in the $1.3520-25 area, while offers were lurking near $1.3500. One possible resistance point for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lies near $1.3525, the bottom of the weekly Ichimoku cloud.\par \par \uc2 "Our economists believe that a satisfactory fiscal compact agreed upon at the summit next week should open the door to ECB quantitative easing, which we expect could come as soon as Q1 2012," wrote analysts at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But analysts at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  warned a lack of progress on the root causes of the euro zone crisis will translate into wider bond yield spreads and a weaker euro in January.\par \par \uc2 With the euro on the front foot for now, the dollar index\par \par \uc2 slipped 0.1 percent to 78.310. Against the yen, the dollar was mildly bid at 77.77, still hemmed in a 77-78 range with investors wary of more massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japan.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar tested this week's high set on Wednesday at $0.7824 for the third time in three straight sessions, but came slightly short of it, last changing hands at 0.7806. It has risen nearly 4 percent this week.\par \par \uc2 Support for the kiwi is seen around $0.7730, while the 55-day moving average at $0.7900 is likely to cap the topside.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar stood at $1.0221, not far off a three-week peak of $1.0335 set earlier in the week.\par \par \uc2 Key resistance is seen around $1.0337, a level representing a 61.8 percent retracement of the November decline.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Masayuki Kitano in Singapore and Ian Chua in Sydney; Editing by Joseph Radford)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111130e7bu00187\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
