\par\par \b \uc2 Aussie, kiwi surge on yen as Japan  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 475 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2011\par 02:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  WELLINGTON/SYDNEY, Oct 31 (Reuters) - The Australian and \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars jumped against the yen on Monday as Japan's authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to push their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2  * Aussie leaped 2.9 pct to a three-month high at 83.50 yen\par \par \uc2 , while the NZ dollar climbed 3.3 pct to 64.34 yen\par \par \uc2 .\par \par \uc2  * With the Japanese concentrating on buying US dollars for yen, the Aussie and kiwi eased back on their U.S. counterpart.\par \par \uc2  * Aussie lower at $1.0615 , from $1.0713 on Friday in New York. It hit a two-month high of $1.0753 last week. Aussie still on target for a gain of around 9.9 pct in October, which would be the second biggest on record.\par \par \uc2  * New Zealand dollar edges back to $0.8170, after last Friday's six-week high of $0.8242. It is on target for a monthly gain of 7.4 percent, which would be the largest since May 2009, following a near 11 percent loss in September.\par \par \uc2  * Support at $0.8166 and then $0.8110, with $0.8242 the first hurdle higher ahead of $0.8290.\par \par \uc2  * Investors focused on RBA policy meeting on Tuesday and quarterly monetary statement on Friday, with a majority of analysts expecting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will cut rates by 25 basis points to 4.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  * The interbank market implies around an 82 percent chance of an easing to 4.5 pct this week <0#YIB:>.\par \par \uc2  * The Aussie trims gains against the kiwi at NZ$1.3000 from early levels around NZ$1.3047. Last week it hit a 4-1/2 month high of NZ$1.3125.\par \par \uc2  * Speculators increase net long positions for Aussie and kiwi, but cut U.S. dollar long bets to the lowest in six weeks. See \par \par \uc2  * Australia has house prices, building approvals and retail sales data this week.  \par \par \uc2  * This week sees NZ third-quarter labour market data, which is expected to show a modest lift in wages and jobs, but little improvement on the 6.5 percent jobless rate. \par \par \uc2  * NZ new home building consents slumped 17.1 percent in September on month before, the first fall in three months, and pointing to wind going out of NZ's economic sails.\par \par \uc2  * Lots of other major events this week with the Federal Reserve meeting Tuesday and Wednesday and the ECB on Thursday. The G20 meets late in the week while US payrolls loom on Friday.\par \par \uc2  * New Zealand government bond prices <0#NZTSY=> a touch firmer with yields down 1.5 basis points along the curve. \par \par \uc2  * Australian debt futures gain, with the three-year contract <YTTc1> 0.04 points higher at 96.08 and the 10-year <YTCc1> up 0.020 points at 95.440. \par \par \uc2 (Australia/New Zealand bureaux)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-AUSTRALIA/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111103e7av0001r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
