\par\par \b \uc2 DJ INTERVIEW: NZ Opposition Pins Long-Shot Hopes On Opposing State Asset Sales\b0\par\par\uc2 518 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2011\par 02:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 -- NZ Labour Party's Trevor Mallard vows to fight Prime Minister Key on proposed government asset sales\par \par \uc2 -- Hiving off high-returning assets is "bloody silly;" public warming to capital-gains tax, Mallard says\par \par \uc2 -- The government should consider changing the RBNZ's mandate to fight the "too high" Kiwi dollar, he says \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By William Mallard \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 AUCKLAND (Dow Jones)-New Zealand's opposition is staking its long-shot election hopes on tarring Prime Minister John Key's government with an unpopular plan to sell stakes in state-owned companies, a leading Labour Party official said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Labour's shadow leader in the House, Trevor Mallard, who held various Cabinet posts between 1999 and 2005, vowed to make inroads by focusing attention on Key's plan to sell stakes of companies such as \f28 \uc2 \f2 Air New Zealand Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (AIR.NZ) and electric-power utilities, while offering as an alternative Labour's proposal to introduce a capital-gains tax.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Key is "an enormous asset" for his center-right National Party, Mallard told Dow Jones in an interview Friday. But the prime minister is "much more popular than his party."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The plan to raise up to NZ$7 billion in asset sales in unpopular. Both main parties agree on the goal of returning the budget to surplus over the next four years, but Mallard said the asset sales are a "bloody silly" idea because the companies have generated 17% average annual returns for the past five years. Labour's internal polls show its tax plan gaining popularity on the grounds of fairness in the months since the party began discussing it, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mallard insisted Labour form a coalition after the Nov. 26 election if it picks up 5-6 points and the Greens hold steady. "The odds are such that you wouldn't bet the house," but they're better than Samoa's chances when it faced mighty Australia in a July rugby match, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Samoa beat the Wallabies 32-23 in a huge upset.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A survey Thursday in The New Zealand Herald showed the National Party with support of 53.8%, on track to form a government by itself, with Labour at 29.5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mallard, Labour's associate finance spokesman, said the "too high" New Zealand dollar is "doing enormous damage to our export industries" and that the government should consider altering the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's mandate to help curb the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 New Zealand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has almost never \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  since the Kiwi was floated in 1985, and Mallard agreed that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is "not a real long-term strategy." But he said the government needs to "give serious consideration to other tools," such as the RBNZ's inflation target or measures of money supply.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The New Zealand dollar, which hit a nearly 30-year high last month above 84 U.S. cents, is trading Monday at US$0.8188.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By William Mallard, Dow Jones Newswires; billy.mallard@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 11, 2011 20:19 ET (00:19 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110912e79c0003s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
