\par\par \b \uc2 REFILE- \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro, commodity  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  down on risk aversion\b0\par\par\uc2 718 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 2011\par 07:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  (Refiles to add missing word "which" to paragraph on \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar)\par \par \uc2  * Swissy, yen broadly flat vs dollar on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears\par \par \uc2  * Commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  skid on risk aversion\par \par \uc2  * Greenback briefly spikes vs yen on stop-loss buying \par \par \uc2  By Antoni Slodkowski\par \par \uc2  TOKYO  Aug 19 (Reuters) - The euro and commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Australian dollar came under pressure on Friday after a raft of weak U.S. economic data and concerns about European banks sent skittish investors piling into U.S. Treasuries and away from stocks.\par \par \uc2  Mounting fears of global recession sent Asian bourses down after the Nasdaq plunged more than 5 percent and European stocks dropped sharply, dragged lower by banks, while safe-haven gold\par \par \uc2 posted record highs above $1.840 per ounce.\par \par \uc2  The fall in the Aussie, however, was limited as it held above its 200-day moving average, with traders citing support from fund and exporter selling of the dollar against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  "I'm really surprised by how quiet the market is today, compared to stocks. Investors are awaiting more developments in Europe and the U.S. as we enjoy some lukewarm pre-weekend trading in Asia," said a trader for a major Japanese bank who did not want to be identified by name.\par \par \uc2   The euro fell 0.2 percent to $1.4310, after slipping 0.8 percent to $1.4303 as an unnamed euro zone bank borrowed $500 million in one-week from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  That sent interbank lending rates soaring with the USD Libor/OIS spread blowing out to 19 basis points, the highest level in 12 months. Three-month Libor also struck four-month highs at 2.9778 percent.\par \par \uc2  "In this situation where we are becoming more concerned about funding, it is natural that the U.S. dollar does well," said Robert Rennie, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Westpac\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 "We are still within a very well rehearsed range of $1.41-$1.45," he added.\par \par \uc2  The dollar was also up 0.1 percent against the New Zealand dollar, which was at $0.8218 after at one point dropping nearly 2 percent to $0.8196. The Aussie was down 0.1 percent at $1.0359 after skidding to $1.0338 from this week's high above $1.0600.\par \par \uc2  "Investors are parking funds in dollars as global economic concerns heighten after weak U.S. data the previous day and as a lack of clarity about Europe's debt problems spreads to banks in the euro zone," said Tsutomu Soma, senior manager at \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  Recent data heightened concerns that the United States will head back into recession at the same time the country's rising debt load and record deficit leave the Federal Reserve with fewer options to stimulate growth.     \par \par \uc2  The data sparked a frenzy of safe-haven buying with 10-year Treasury yields hitting a deep low of 1.97 percent. They were last at 2.033 percent.\par \par \uc2  Investors refrained from shifting into traditional safe havens such as the Swiss franc and the yen due to fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  Market players were reminded about the looming \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the dollar briefly spiked against the yen hitting as high as 77 yen on stop-loss buying with talk of buying from a British bank.\par \par \uc2  STAYING ON GUARD\par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities also warned traders to keep their guard up after Switzerland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the forward market earlier this week and as it engineers negative interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/yen is expected to be supported, but gains will be limited at 77 yen. It should gradually test downside below a record low 76.25," Okasan's Soma said.\par \par \uc2  Soma's views echo those of other Tokyo traders who warn that as the Japanese return from their summer holidays next week, exporters will likely sell dollar/yen in end-of-the-month transactions, heightening the risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  The dollar last traded at 76.53 yen and stayed almost unchanged versus the Swiss franc at 0.7940, having risen to 0.7944 franc from 0.7822. Against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  , it was 0.1 percent higher at 74.33, compared to Thursday's open at 73.77.    \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Chikafumi Hodo and Cecile Lefort; Editing by Joseph Radford)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (CORRECTED)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110819e78j0008m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
