\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-NZ monetary policy changes not needed, NZ$ hurts-FinMin\b0\par\par\uc2 378 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2011\par 05:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  (Adds quotes, background)\par \par \uc2  * High NZ dollar hurting exports side of economy\par \par \uc2  * Different \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy not needed\par \par \uc2  * \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  RBNZ decision, doubts of success \par \par \uc2  WELLINGTON, Aug 4 (Reuters) - The high New Zealand dollar is hurting exporters and recovery but significant changes to the country's monetary policy are neither needed nor planned, the finance minister said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2  "Of course the high dollar is difficult for our exporters, we would much prefer the dollar to be lower," Bill English said in reply to a question in parliament.\par \par \uc2  "But there is no obvious monetary policy tool that could achieve that."\par \par \uc2  The New Zealand dollar fell sharply against the U.S. dollar on Thursday, tracking the yen down after the Bank of Japan bought the greenback to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  It last traded around $0.8535 after falling from $0.8640 when the Bank of Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the strength of the Japanese yen. For more see \par \par \uc2  "We would prefer a lower exchange rate, the world's not delivering that to us, so we have to get on and build a competitive economy," English added.\par \par \uc2  The kiwi's fall was given added momentum when English separately told the Dow Jones Newswires that a high local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a headwind for the economy.\par \par \uc2  English repeated any potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to bring down the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar would be up to the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but said the experience of other central banks in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been less than successful.\par \par \uc2  Tim Kelleher, CBA's vice president of institutional banking and markets, said the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  also triggered some stop-loss selling in the kiwi.\par \par \uc2  "The main driver was the Japanese buying (U.S. dollar)," Kelleher said.\par \par \uc2  The New Zealand dollar is up almost 10 percent so far this year, denting the competitiveness of the country's exports.\par \par \uc2  Earlier this week, Prime Minister John Key, a former head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealing at \uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2 , said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the RBNZ was unlikely to be successful because it had not worked overseas and the New Zealand economy continued to be seen as more attractive than the US economy.\par \par \uc2 (Gyles Beckford)\par \par \uc2 NEWZEALAND-ECONOMY/FINMIN (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110807e7840001x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
