\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro at record low vs safe haven Swiss franc\b0\par\par\uc2 708 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juni 2011\par 02:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Euro under pressure as Greek debt saga rolls on\par \par \uc2 * Hits record low vs Swiss franc <EURCHF=> in thin trade\par \par \uc2 * Large option structures vulnerable at 1.2000 francs\par \par \uc2 (Adds details, updates prices)\par \par \uc2 By Anirban Nag and Neal Armstrong\par \par \uc2 LONDON, June 13 (Reuters) - The euro hit a record low against the Swiss franc on Monday, as worries over attempts to deal with the Greek debt crisis made investors shy away from the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and seek relative safety in the franc.\par \par \uc2 Volumes were on the lower side with many centres in Europe closed for a holiday. The cost of insuring peripheral euro zone bonds against default hit a record high, while a general lack of appetite towards riskier assets dented euro sentiment.\par \par \uc2 The euro fell to as low as 1.2004 francs <EURCHF=> on trading platform EBS. Traders said stop-loss orders were triggered after it fell through 1.2050, and system-related selling pushed it towards 1.2000 francs in thin liquidity.\par \par \uc2 They highlighted large option structures at 1.2000, with a break below expected to trigger more euro selling. Euro/Swiss last traded around 1.2025, down around 0.6 percent for the day.\par \par \uc2 The euro <EUR=> also skidded to a one-week low in Asia of $1.4285 before some \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  demand helped it pare losses. It was last trading with slight gains on the day at $1.4383.\par \par \uc2 Resistance was at the 55-day moving average at $1.4398 and a concentration of former support levels around $1.4430/50, which includes previous intraday peaks in May and a 38.2 percent retracement of the May-to-June rise.\par \par \uc2 "What the euro needs is a resolution to the Greek crisis and the politicians and the central bankers do not appear to be close to finding one," said Kit Juckes, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 . "That uncertainty is weighing on the euro and I expect it to be stuck in a $1.40-$1.47 range."\par \par \uc2 European policymakers appeared deadlocked on how private investors could be involved in some form of restructuring of Greek debt. The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is opposed to German proposals for a bond swap, arguing that it would trigger market turmoil.\par \par \uc2 Bundesbank head Jens Weidmann in comments released at the weekend said he was against extending the maturities of Greek bonds held by the ECB in any "soft" restructuring.\par \par \uc2 German and French banks were leaning towards contributing to a Greek rescue, even as it remained unclear how they could do so without triggering a default or credit default swap contracts. [ID:nLDE75A065]\par \par \uc2 Investors also see less risk of repeated euro zone rate hikes in the months ahead. Even as ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet signalled a rate hike is on the way in July, the ECB left inflation forecasts for 2012 little changed, prompting traders to slash expectations for higher policy rates.\par \par \uc2 The euro's uncertain outlook saw implied volatilities rise with risk reversals also widening, suggesting more downside risks for the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . <EURVOL=ICAP>\par \par \uc2 SAFE HAVEN FLOWS\par \par \uc2 The euro's woes have been compounded by a drop in global stocks that has prompted some market players to unwind carry trades funded with the U.S. dollar, the yen and the Swiss franc. World stocks slumped to a 12-week low on Monday. <.MIWD00000PUS>\par \par \uc2 The rush towards safe-haven assets boosted the Swiss franc and kept the dollar pinned near record lows hit last week. The dollar was last down 0.7 percent at 0.8374 <CHF=>.\par \par \uc2 "The Swiss franc remains the global barometer for risk and the fresh euro zone jitters have given renewed confidence to investors to push it higher," said Lena Komileva, head of G-10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  meets this week for a rate decision, and is unlikely to signal any risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the franc's strength.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar tumbled after a series of powerful earthquakes shook Christchurch, four months after the city was badly damaged by a 6.3 magnitude quake. The kiwi dropped 1 percent to trade at $0.8125 <NZD=D4>.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Catherine Evans)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 4)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110613e76d0001b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
