\par\par \b \uc2 DJ 2nd UPDATE: RBNZ Stands Pat; No Rate Hike Tipped Until March\b0\par\par\uc2 872 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 April 2011\par 03:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 -- RBNZ keeps its key rate at 2.5%, cites post-quake uncertainties\par \par \uc2 -- Gov. Bollard says the current rate looks "appropriate for some time"\par \par \uc2 -- Bollard says high oil prices, Kiwi dollar unwelcome\par \par \uc2 -- RBNZ tips on-year inflation easing back into target band after tax impact drops out\par \par \uc2 (Recasts lead, adds context and analysis throughout, snap poll in fourth paragraph, updates New Zealand dollar reaction in 12th paragraph.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   By Rebecca Howard \par   Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 WELLINGTON (Dow Jones)--New Zealand's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  left interest rates unchanged Thursday and, despite rising inflation, indicated it won't tighten policy for some time as the economy fights to recover from a devastating earthquake.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The head of the Reserve Bank of New Zealand also expressed concern about the strength of the local dollar, but economists say that unlike its counterparts in much of Asia, the RBNZ almost certainly won't \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The outlook for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 New Zealand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  economy remains very uncertain following February's Christchurch earthquake," Reserve Bank of New Zealand Gov. Alan Bollard said in a statement after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  left its Official Cash Rate at 2.5%. "Given the outlook for core inflation and continued economic disruption stemming from the earthquakes, the current level of the OCR is likely to remain appropriate for some time."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The median forecast for the RBNZ to raise rates, in a Dow Jones survey of economists after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  decision, is for March, unchanged from a poll a few days ago. Thursday's 14 forecast dates for a rate increase range from December to the second quarter of 2012. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's last policy change was a half-percentage-point cut in March in response to the magnitude-6.3 quake.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The RBNZ acknowledged some signs of improvement, but appears in no hurry to remove policy support," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  economist Philip Borkin. "Given contained underlying inflation, a steep yield curve and the current profile of mortgage borrowers, we believe the RBNZ will remain more patient when it comes to tightening than it has possibly shown in the past."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Feb. 22 quake, which killed more than 160 people, destroyed much of the country's second-largest city, just six months after another big quake in Christchurch.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Even as the economy suffers from the aftermath of the disasters, inflation is getting uncomfortably high. Consumer prices rose 4.5% from a year earlier in the first quarter, above the RBNZ's 1%-3% target band.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But this was largely due to an October increase in the goods-and-services tax and recent tobacco and cigarette tax hikes. Bollard said New Zealand's "annual inflation is expected to settle comfortably within the target band once these tax increases drop out of the annual rate."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ASB economist Jane Turner said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "remains very comfortable on the inflation outlook," as "underlying inflation pressures are currently very subdued, allowing the RBNZ plenty of breathing room over the next year."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chief said growth among New Zealand's trading partners remains robust, elevating prices for the country's commodity exports, but that higher oil prices and the elevated level of the New Zealand dollar are unwelcome and "they will have some dampening effect on economic activity."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The New Zealand dollar fell to US$0.8007 from US$0.8078 in the 20 minutes after Bollard's statement before rebounding on a broad slide in the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . At 0132 GMT the Kiwi dollar was at US$0.8053.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although Bollard is displeased with the New Zealand dollar's rise to three-year highs against the greenback, "the conditions for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are definitely not there," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 ANZ Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  senior economist Khoon Goh. The Kiwi isn't reaching near-term highs on a trade-weighted index, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Part of the driver for the higher Kiwi is a rampant Aussie and generalized U.S. dollar weakness, and there is not really a lot that the Reserve Bank can do about that," Goh said. "It is a very risky strategy to undertake unilateral \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the current environment anyway."\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 New Zealand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's economic growth in the first quarter is expected to be very weak, given the extensive damage to infrastructure, buildings and residences in the Christchurch area on the country's South Island. Government officials have said about a quarter of the buildings in the city center will likely have to be demolished and thousands of homes may need to be rebuilt. In March, the Treasury estimated the quake will cut about 1.5 percentage points off this year's growth rate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bollard reiterated that business confidence, consumer spending and tourism all declined sharply following the earthquake but he was cautiously optimistic about activity outside the Christchurch area.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While many businesses and households in Christchurch remain affected, in the rest of the country confidence and consumer spending have shown some signs of recovery, he said, adding that housing market turnover and business investment are beginning to increase.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Rebecca Howard, Dow Jones Newswires; 64-4-471-5990; rebecca.howard@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 April 27, 2011 21:32 ET (01:32 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110428e74s000ea\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
