\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECT: NZ Agricultural Export Prices Likely To Remain Strong\b0\par\par\uc2 758 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 April 2011\par 04:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 ("UPDATE: NZ Agricultural Export Prices Likely To Remain Strong," at 0112 GMT, misquoted a \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  note in the 6th paragraph. The correct version follows:)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Rebecca Howard and Lucy Craymer \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON (Dow Jones)--New Zealand's agricultural export prices are likely to remain strong for some time, delivering benefits to the New Zealand economy, the Reserve Bank of New Zealand said in a statement released Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 According to the statement, Reserve Bank of New Zealand Gov. Alan Bollard told a farming group, The Grasshoppers, late Monday that the bank expects the higher terms of trade to continue to be reflected in the exchange rate, as it is currently.\par \par \uc2 "The exchange rate would deliver the benefits of the rising terms of trade to the community at large--through higher wealth and cheaper imports," Bollard said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists see these comments as quashing the possibility that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would look at \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar as it trades around a five-month high.\par \par \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  said in an analyst note that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be contemplated by \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the weak economic situation currently being confronted is quite different to situations of the past.\par \par \uc2 "In our opinion, the current environment is such that \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can be contemplated in a manner that is credible, consistent with the RBNZ's monetary policy objectives and not injurious to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's balance sheet," it said.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Bank of New Zealand\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Mike Jones said his bank's view is that RBNZ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to lower the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar was "very unlikely" and this was supported by Bollard's comments that the exchange rate would deliver the benefits of the rising terms of trade to the community at large.\par \par \uc2 "It's also hard to argue the New Zealand dollar is unjustified relative to fundamentals," Jones said. "NZ's current account is still in deficit, but not by nearly the same extent as when the RBNZ last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Bollard added in his speech that monetary policy will remain focused on any medium-term inflationary pressures that arise, rather than the shift in the terms of trade itself.\par \par \uc2 However, "if households and firms use the income boost from higher commodity prices and exchange rates to bring forward consumption and investment, or increase borrowing, then pressure on resources in New Zealand would lead to more inflationary pressure," he said.\par \par \uc2 "Monetary policy would need to counteract any rise in inflation expectations," Bollard added.\par \par \uc2 Bollard's comments have been interpreted as slightly hawkish, with the swaps markets adding 11 basis points to where it expects to see the official cash rate in 12 months' time, said \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  Senior Strategist Roland Randall.\par \par \uc2 "The market made a more hawkish interpretation of Dr. Bollard's comments than we think is warranted," Randall said.\par \par \uc2 The New Zealand economy has remained sluggish and the Reserve Bank of New Zealand cut the official cash rate by 50 basis points on March 10 in an effort to boost confidence and increase spending following the devastating Feb. 22 earthquake.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday simultaneously released analysis, in a report entitled "The Outlook For Commodity Prices And Implications For New Zealand Monetary Policy." The report looks at what the boom in the terms of trade should mean to New Zealand's economy and in turn the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rate policy.\par \par \uc2 "Monetary policy should remain focused on the medium-term objective of price stability," the report said. It added that while focusing on medium-term inflation, policy makers will need to be cautious that the boom doesn't lead to increases in inflation expectations.\par \par \uc2 "Should households and firms decide the income boost from higher commodity prices and exchange rates enables them to bring forward consumption and investment or increase borrowing, it is possible an internal demand shock will arise," the report said.\par \par \uc2 "Consequent pressure on resources within New Zealand would lead to more inflationary pressure and monetary policy would counteract any rise in inflation expectations," it said.\par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of New Zealand is required to maintain inflation within a band of 1.0% to 3.0%, although it looks through one-off price rises such as those related to the government's decision to increase the goods and services tax to 15% from 12.5% on Oct. 1 last year.\par \par \uc2 -By Rebecca Howard and Lucy Craymer, Dow Jones Newswires; 64-4-471-5990; lucy.craymer@dowjones.com [ 12-04-11 0248GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110412e74c00060\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
