\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Asian Shares Mostly Higher; Techs Lead In Tokyo, Seoul\b0\par\par\uc2 1,199 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 2010\par 06:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds information, quotes, updates/adds market levels)\par \par \uc2 By Shri Navaratnam and Ga-Woon Philip Vahn\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian stock markets were mostly higher Friday with technology plays leading gains in Tokyo and Seoul, while weakness in China's banking stocks dragged the Shanghai market lower.\par \par \uc2 Investors were encouraged by a third-straight session of gains on Wall Street on Thursday, as well an unexpected increase in U.S. pending-home sales. However, buying was tempered by caution ahead of the U.S. non-farm payrolls report for August due later Friday.\par \par \uc2 "U.S. economic data have been surprising, more than less, on the upside this week," said Philip Wee at DBS Group Research in Singapore. That could be because of "U.S. double-dip worries injecting more-than-usual bearishness into the consensus forecasts."\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei Stock Average was up 0.4% while Australia's S&P/ASX 200 rose 0.2%, South Korea's Kospi Composite gained 0.1% and India's Sensex was 0.2% higher.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index was flat, Taiwan's main index gained 1.1% and the Shanghai Composite index was down 0.2%. Dow Jones Industrial Average futures were two points lower in screen trade.\par \par \uc2 The Tokyo market was cheered by Wall Street's rise, with tech stocks outperforming after the Nasdaq Composite rose 1.1% Thursday.\par \par \uc2 However, the market's "gains may be limited since the yen's strength, which is our biggest concern, shows no signs of receding," said Kazuhiro Takahashi, general manager at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets. "We'd like to see how the dollar moves against the yen after U.S. jobs data later today," he said.\par \par \uc2 Elpida Memory rose 3.2%, \uc2 Toshiba\uc2  advanced 1.3% and \uc2 Sony\uc2  tacked on 1.7%. Automakers were also rising, with \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  up 1.4% and \uc2 Nissan Motor\uc2  0.6% higher.\par \par \uc2 In Seoul, shares were supported by the positive sentiment in markets.\par \par \uc2 "The U.S. market has turned around from the short-term lows after the ISM (manufacturing) data Wednesday, so the domestic market's moving quickly in line as well," said \uc2 Hyundai Securities\uc2  analyst Bae Sung-young.\par \par \uc2 Tech stocks were broadly higher, aided by an easing of concerns about global growth prospects. \uc2 LG Electronics\uc2  rose 3.2%, \uc2 Hynix Semiconductor\uc2  added 2.8% and LG Display advanced 3.1%.\par \par \uc2 Shinhan Financial fell 2.0% on worries about the company's internal governance as the board took steps to remove President and Chief Executive Shin Sang-hoon from his posts.\par \par \uc2 Shinhan Bank\uc2 , a unit of Shinhan Financial, said on Thursday it filed a complaint with prosecutors against Shin Sang-hoon and six others and accused them of breach of trust as well as embezzlement.\par \par \uc2 Banks were struggling in Shanghai after the China Securities Journal said, citing its own statistics, that four of China's five biggest banks reported increases in special-mention loans in the second quarter from the preceding quarter, offering fresh evidence of potentially high credit risk in the local banking system. Bank of China fell 0.6% and \uc2 China Merchants Bank\uc2  lost 0.8%.\par \par \uc2 Special-mention loans refer to loans that have a potential weakness or pose an unwarranted financial risk that, if not corrected, could weaken the asset and lead to higher risks.\par \par \uc2 China Vanke\uc2  rose 0.9% after the company said its property sales in August more than doubled from a year earlier to CNY11.99 billion, a record monthly performance for a Chinese property developer.\par \par \uc2 The Sydney market was led higher by gains in materials, industrials and consumer staples.\par \par \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  was up 0.4% and BHP Billiton rose 0.3%. Bank stocks were mostly lower, capping the market's gains. ANZ bank was off 0.7%, \uc2 Westpac\uc2  was off 1.0% and \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  fell 1.1%.\par \par \uc2 Nufarm fell 3.1% after \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  placed the company's BB long-term corporate debt rating on negative credit watch, on concerns about liquidity and its ability to improve profitability in fiscal 2011.\par \par \uc2 In Hong Kong, \uc2 Sino Land\uc2  rose 2.3% after the blue-chip property developer said Thursday its net profit for the 12 months ended June 30 rose 63% to HK$6.09 billion, helped by revaluation gains in its investment properties.\par \par \uc2 Cement shares were leading gains in Thailand on hopes that construction activity will pick up after a Thai court Thursday allowed the resumption of almost all the projects at a controversial industrial estate, almost a year after they were suspended. \uc2 Siam Cement\uc2  rose 5.0%, Banpu gained 1.6% and PTT Chemical added 3.4%.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, Malaysian shares were down 0.5%, Singapore's Straits Times Index fell 0.1%, New Zealand's NZX-50 was up 0.6% and Indonesia's share market was 0.9% higher. Vietnam markets were shut for a public holiday.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the U.S. dollar traded in tight ranges against the euro and the yen as traders waited for the U.S. non-farm payrolls data.\par \par \uc2 The euro was at $1.2815, from $1.2821 late Thursday in New York, and at Y107.99 against the yen, from Y107.96. The dollar was fetching Y84.26, compared with Y84.21.\par \par \uc2 "With investors still on edge about the chance of a double-dip U.S. recession, we suspect any disappointment (on the U.S. jobs data) would scupper the recent bond market sell-off, providing headwinds for the U.S. dollar," said Mike Jones, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Friday after the Singapore dollar hit an all-time high against the greenback, a person familiar with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's thinking said.\par \par \uc2 The person told Dow Jones Newswires that the MAS "went in to smooth the U.S. dollar's fall around S$1.3435."\par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar hit a record high late Thursday, with the U.S. dollar sliding to S$1.3425. The previous record was S$1.3438 to the greenback in July 2008. The U.S. dollar last traded at S$1.3471.\par \par \uc2 Lead September Japanese government bond futures were down 0.01 at 142.20 points, weighed by the Nikkei's gains and Thursday's weakness in U.S. Treasurys. The 10-year cash JGB yield was 0.5 basis point lower at 1.105%.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold was at $1,249.50 per troy ounce, down $1.80 from late New York Thursday. October Nymex crude oil futures were down 26 cents at $74.76 per barrel on Globex.\par \par \uc2 -Shri Navaratnam, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4140; shri.navaratnam@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 03-09-10 0401GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1106 4125 5037 62560   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100903e69300072\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgo