\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-INTERVIEW-UPDATE 2-NZ dlr should reflect fundamentals-finmin\b0\par\par\uc2 561 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Oktober 2009\par 20:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 * NZ's English says wishes NZD reflects economy, not flows\par \par \uc2 * Sees China playing greater FDI role in country\par \par \uc2 (Recasts with Reuters interview, adds details)\par \par \uc2 By Eric Burroughs\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Oct 5 (Reuters) - New Zealand Finance Minister Bill English said on Monday that he wished the country's strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reflected its economic fundamentals rather than \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market flows.\par \par \uc2 English also said that the government was trying to help the economy by getting its finances into shape.\par \par \uc2 "I wish it would reflect economic fundamentals rather than trade currents in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," English told Reuters in an interview on Monday when asked about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar <NZD=D4> has soared 23 percent to a 14-month high against the U.S. dollar this year despite the country suffering its deepest recession on record, getting a boost from investor demand for higher-yielding, commodity-linked \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the world economy recovers.\par \par \uc2 The government and policymakers have repeatedly said the strong kiwi is not helpful for the country's exports but partly reflects the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's broad slide.\par \par \uc2 English said that New Zealand's exporters have had to get by with a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for much of the past four years but that the government can best help by getting its finances into shape. The country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is in charge of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  emerged from its longest recession on record in the second quarter but growth was a meagre 0.1 percent.\par \par \uc2 "We are realistic about how testing this recovery could be," English said.\par \par \uc2 English also said New Zealand's interest rates were likely to remain low when compared with neighbouring Australia.\par \par \uc2 "Our interest rates are likely to be lower for longer than for instance Australia," English told CNBC television earlier in the day.\par \par \uc2 The Reserve \uc2 Bank of New Zealand\uc2  has slashed rates to a record low 2.50 percent and pledged to keep them there or lower until the latter part of 2010 to help the economy.\par \par \uc2 The RBNZ is expected to leave rates unchanged at its next review on Oct. 29, according to a Reuters poll, with investors eyeing a first rate hike early in 2010. [NZ/POLL]\par \par \uc2 The Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holds its October policy meeting on Tuesday and most analysts expect it to keep rates at 3.0 percent [AU/INT], though two influential economic columnists say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to raise rates to 3.25 percent. [ID:nSYD44556]\par \par \uc2 English is meeting investors in Hong Kong and London to promote New Zealand, which relies heavily on foreign borrowing to fund its current account deficit -- totalling nearly 100 percent of the country's economy -- due to low levels of household savings.\par \par \uc2 The current account gap is a vulnerability, and the country's need to attract foreign capital may mean that China plays a greater role in providing foreign direct investment, English said.\par \par \uc2 "They are a likely to become more significant investors in New Zealand," he said.\par \par \uc2 English also said that Asian central banks diversifying their reserves and making a small increase in Australasian bonds was making a "big difference" for New Zealand as it tries to lure foreign investors to finance its deficit. (Additional reporting by Mantik Kusjanto in Wellington) (Editing by Kim Coghill)\par \par \uc2 x|LANGEN|ND    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091008e5a5001gt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
