\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET SIGNALS-Asia at forefront of exit strategies, RBNZ lags\b0\par\par\uc2 By Eric Burroughs, Asia Financial Markets Editor   \par \uc2 778 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 2009\par 10:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Sept 7 (Reuters) - The G20 went out of its way to make clear stimulus would not be removed too quickly and it's a little early to start talking exit strategies. Not that surprising and fine for the G8 world, where economic conditions are only just starting to turn the corner.\par \par \uc2 In the other G12, comprising Asia's biggest economies besides Japan, central banks are at the forefront of exit strategies as Australian Treasurer Wayne Swan flagged in remarks last week, despite other cautious remarks sticking to the G20 line in London.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Korea, which meets this week, is up there with counterparts in Australia, China and India among Asian central banks that are the closest to pulling the trigger or taking other monetary measures.\par \par \uc2 Then there is the frustrated Reserve \uc2 Bank of New Zealand\uc2  (RBNZ). It also meets this week and has kept threatening further rate cuts to offset tightening in financial conditions via higher rates and a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RBNZ BETWEEN ROCK AND HARD PLACE\par \par \uc2 For a graphic on RBNZ rates and the New Zealand dollar's trade-weighted index: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/tuk25d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/tuk25d}}}\par \par \uc2 For the RBNZ, despite the threats, it's hard to see what a rate cut would achieve. New Zealand financial conditions are only partially determined by policy rates.\par \par \uc2 New Zealand's plight is not much different from that of other small and open economies in Asia: a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  only has so much influence. In theory, they could print New Zealand dollars <NZD=D4> to try to stymie the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength.\par \par \uc2 But market players have argued the trade-weighted NZD <=NZD> is not at levels to justify \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , noting that its 2007 kiwi-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had little sustained impact.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar has been one of the main \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  held hostage to equity markets via the risky asset correlation trade. That risk-correlation trade is showing signs of weakening, but only just, suggesting that the RBNZ probably has to sit tight and keep talking tough.\par \par \uc2 BOK CLOSER TO TIGHTENING\par \par \uc2 For a graphic comparing different short-term swap curves in Asia: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/bam25d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/bam25d}}}\par \par \uc2 For the BOK, the short-end of the swap curve has been telling. The one-year/three-year swap curve has seen a notable flattening and the timing of a first hike has become clearer. By contrast, the New Zealand curve is holding near its steepest levels on record because a RBNZ shift on policy is expected to start a year out or further.\par \par \uc2 For the Bank of Korea (BOK), asset prices are a worry.\par \par \uc2 The KOSPI <.KS11> has been at the forefront of Asia's equity surge this year on the rapid recovery in manufacturing and tech, making it among the first indexes to reach the 61.8 percent retracement of its entire 2007-08 sell-off.\par \par \uc2 By contrast, the U.S. S&P 500 <.SPX> has only just reached a 38.2 percent retracement.\par \par \uc2 After S.Korea's Financial Supervisory Service (FSS) tightened mortgage lending rules on Friday, the BOK is likely to keep talking tough on asset prices.\par \par \uc2 But a rate hike -- rather than regulatory tinkering -- is only a matter of time. As with the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2 , the timing for a BOK move is seen most likely in the final months of the year.\par \par \uc2 U.S. ECONOMY HEALING, FAR FROM HEALTHY\par \par \uc2 For a graphic comparing the ISM indexes and continuing jobless claims: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/vum25d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/vum25d}}}\par \par \uc2 Still, as many economists have repeatedly warned, the U.S. economy is just starting to heal. Job cuts are slowing, manufacturing has rebounded very quickly and the slide in corporate earnings has bottomed out.\par \par \uc2 But the U.S. economy has a long way to go on the road to recovery. Looking at the spread between the ISM non-manufacturing PMI and the manufacturing one, the underperformance of the services sector -- some 80-85 percent of the economy these days -- is striking.\par \par \uc2 That spread is near its most negative levels since the services PMI began in 1997. The total number of continuing jobless claims gives a glimpse of how many people are out of work.\par \par \uc2 The recession is almost certainly over. But U.S. growth in coming quarters should be spotty and noisy as the stimulus effects kick in.\par \par \uc2 Until the U.S. economic recovery solidifies and is less dependent on stimulus, Asia will tread carefully in tightening policy. (Editing by Neil Fullick)\par \par \uc2 MARKET-SIGNALS/ASIA|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|UKI|J|U|NAT|RNP|DNP|PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090907e597000ic\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
