\par\par \b \uc2 NZ dollar holds near 9-month highs, bonds firmer\b0\par\par\uc2 421 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 2009\par 23:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Kiwi stays near nine-month highs on rising equities\par \par \uc2 * Ignores another government warning on strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Swaps steady, bonds firmer\par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON, July 23 (Reuters) - The New Zealand dollar <NZD=> held near nine-month highs on Thursday as firmer stock markets supported appetite for higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while another government warning against a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had little impact.\par \par \uc2 The kiwi settled around S0.6600/02 at 0515 GMT, compared with $0.6554/59 late on Wednesday. It hit a high of $0.6632 <NZD=D2> in overseas trading, the strongest since the first week of October last year, before retreating.\par \par \uc2 Asian equity markets were higher, including Hong Kong and Japan, which rose over 1 percent, pointing to solid risk appetite.\par \par \uc2 "Equities futures are up at the moment and the kiwi is pushing higher," said \uc2 HSBC\uc2  Treasury chief manager Daniel Brdanovic. "We need follow through from equities to give kiwi the momentum".\par \par \uc2 The New Zealand \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also benefited from a weak U.S. dollar, which hovered close to a seven-week low on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Bill English said New Zealand needed a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bolster exports and should consume less and save more if it is to shake off recession and correct economic imbalances. [ID:nWEL481934]\par \par \uc2 His comments echoed those he made last week in Sydney that the kiwi should be lower based on fundamentals, though he added then that policy makers were not considering market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make that happen.\par \par \uc2 So far this year the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar has risen around 13 percent, and is nearly 35 percent above the six-year lows it fell to in March.\par \par \uc2 "These recent moves higher for the New Zealand dollar are far from favourable for an economy that requires rebalancing," said ANZ-National markets economist Philip Borkin.\par \par \uc2 With little on the domestic agenda this week, investors are already looking ahead to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's interest rate review next week, with expectations for no change to the 2.5 percent cash rate [ID:nWEL57452].\par \par \uc2 Interest rate swaps were little changed, with two-year <NZDSM3NB2Y=> swaps bid at 3.91 percent, and five-year <NZDSM3NB5Y=> at 5.35 percent.\par \par \uc2 New Zealand government bonds were firmer, with yields on the benchmark NZ 10-year bond <NZ10YT=RR> 6 basis points lower at 5.75 percent, recouping previous' day losses. (Reporting by Mantik Kusjanto; Editing by James Thornhill)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-NEWZEALAND-DOLLAR (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|Table|ND|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|ABX|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090725e57m000el\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
