\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-New Zealand c.bank chief says long-term rates too high\b0\par\par\uc2 754 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2009\par 22:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * NZ \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says jump in wholesale rates unjustified\par \par \uc2 * C.bank says expects to keep its rates low for some time\par \par \uc2 * NZ dollar and market interest rates fall sharply (Adds comment, market reaction, background)\par \par \uc2 By Mantik Kusjanto\par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON, April 1 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday to halt a rise in long-term interest rates, saying benchmark rates would stay low for much longer.\par \par \uc2 Reserve \uc2 Bank of New Zealand\uc2  Governor Alan Bollard said the rise in wholesale rates was at odds with the bank's expectations for a slow recovery of the recession-hit economy, with risks to the downside, and with its policy of cutting benchmark rates.\par \par \uc2 "In these circumstances we believe the rise in longer-term interest rates is unwarranted and inconsistent with the monetary policy outlook," he said in a statement that sent the New zealand dollar lower and debt yields tumbling.\par \par \uc2 Last month, Bollard said rates would likely be cut further, but cautioned that they would not go as low as some were expecting because they needed to remain attractive to investors so the country could fund its big current account deficit.\par \par \uc2 That prompted investors to expect an end to the easing cycle and sparked a rush to seize low rates, sending swap and mortgage rates sharply higher and forcing the RBNZ to talk things down.\par \par \uc2 "It was an appropriate response given the irrationality we have seen in the market," said ANZ-National Bank chief economist Cameron Bagrie.\par \par \uc2 The verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  sent the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar down more than 2 percent to a low of $0.5558, from around $0.5700. Bank bill futures rose sharply and the yields on government bonds settled around 8 basis points lower along the curve.\par \par \uc2 Five-year interest rate swaps, which had leaped more than 50 basis points in the past week, sank to 4.755/4.835 percent from 5.07/15 percent.\par \par \uc2 WILD SWINGS\par \par \uc2 The swaps market has seen wild swings over the past two weeks as banks rushed to hedge a wave of households renewing their expiring home loans at cheaper fixed rates.\par \par \uc2 At one point, five-year swap rates notched up their biggest weekly rise since 1994, while two-year swaps had their largest weekly increase since 1998.\par \par \uc2 The jump in yields in turn added to upward pressure on the kiwi dollar, making exports less competitive at a time when global trade is already reeling.\par \par \uc2 Bollard said if the distortion in rates and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued it would put needless pressure on companies' and households' borrowing costs.\par \par \uc2 "As indicated in our March statement, we are projecting interest rates to remain at relatively low levels for an extended period," he said.\par \par \uc2 The RBNZ has cut interest rates to a record-low 3 percent from a high of 8.25 percent since last July, and signalled in its March 12 monetary statement that it expected rates to go to around 2.5 percent, and possibly as low as 2 percent if global conditions justified it.\par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll has analysts expecting the RBNZ cutting its cash rate by at least 25 basis points at its April 30 review, with a similar-sized cut in June.\par \par \uc2 OPTIONS\par \par \uc2 Some analysts have suggested the RBNZ could take more exotic action such as follow the lead of the U.S. Treasury and buy back long-dated government debt to pump money into the system.\par \par \uc2 Bank of New Zealand\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Danica Hampton said quantitative easing was one option for the bank but also cautioned that the impact may be limited by the lack of scope and depth of New Zealand's capital market.\par \par \uc2 "If New Zealand were to launch a U.S.-style quantitative easing programme, what assets would the RBNZ or NZ Treasury buy?" she said, adding that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could enter the swap market directly to stabilise rates.\par \par \uc2 David Troy, an interest rate strategist at ANZ, said the volatility was likely to be temporary and would pass, making it important for the RBNZ to hold to its strategy.\par \par \uc2 "There is a limit to how low the OCR (official cash rate) can go. However, there is no limit to how long it can stay low."\par \par \uc2 "With the economy yet to trough, market pricing of higher rates and a turn in the cycle is misplaced." (Editing by Jan Dahinten)\par \par \uc2 NEWZEALAND-ECONOMY/RBNZ (UPDATE 3)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090331e53v001p4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
