\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ FOCUS: Rampant Rise In NZ Dlr, Rates May Spur RBNZ To Action\b0\par\par\uc2 By Simon Louisson Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 1,314 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2009\par 03:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2009, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON (Dow Jones)--A sharp bounce in the New Zealand dollar and a spike in market interest rates have dramatically tightened monetary conditions in what may force a Reserve \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  response as radical as \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the interest rate swaps market.\par \par \uc2 The sudden reversal in market conditions could tighten the noose on businesses and consumers already suffering from the country's worst recession in decades and has likely caught the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  off guard. As recently as its March 12 quarterly review of economic and monetary conditions, the RBNZ signaled that the Official Cash Rate, at 3%, could trough at 2.5% midyear and then rise quite steeply in 2010.\par \par \uc2 That view was based on expectations that a plunge in the New Zealand dollar over the past year coupled with a series of deep rate cuts since July would eventually spur a recovery.\par \par \uc2 But last week's move by the U.S. Federal Reserve to escalate its quantitative easing program has altered the landscape. The New Zealand dollar has surged 17% since March 10, a sizable part of this rally spurred by the Fed's drastic move to print money to help ease liquidity. At the same time, 10-year New Zealand dollar swap rates reversed a yearlong precipitous plunge, rising to 5.80% from 4.80%, driven by corporate demand for credit and international influences, prompting banks to raise mortgage lending rates.\par \par \uc2 'The RBNZ is on notice here,' said \uc2 ANZ National Bank\uc2  chief economist Cameron Bagrie. 'They are going to have to flag here that rates are going to have to remain low here for an extended period.'\par \par \uc2 New Zealand has been in recession for more than a year, and recent data show the downturn has deepened as the global economic slump has combined with a domestic-led housing slowdown to crunch consumption. Businesses continue to battle falling profits and job losses are expected to mount this year.\par \par \uc2 Warren Potter, fixed-interest portfolio manager with \uc2 AMP Capital Investors\uc2 , said the Kiwi dollar's rebound to around 57 U.S. cents and possible further gains, in conjunction with rising mortgage rates despite the RBNZ's 50 basis-point rate cut March 12, threatens to prolong the recession.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par        Jawbone Rates Lower \par   Potter said the RBNZ first ought to 'jawbone' rates lower, starting before the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's next rate review April 30. 'That would have the effect of lowering front-end rates, and maybe reduce pressure for banks to continue to lift their mortgage rates,' he said. \par   If that fails, then the bank, which has already slashed the cash rate 525 basis points since July, ought to consider cutting its cash rate April 30 by more than the 25 basis points the market now anticipates. \par   'That would result in a dramatic move down in front-end rates,' said Potter. \par   Bagrie said the game has changed since the move by the Fed, which apart from escalating its quantitative easing also flagged its lending rates would remain near zero for an extended period. \par   'At the moment the local market is expecting rates to rise in 2010,' Bagrie said. \par   'That is just la-la stuff given the underlying state of this economy and given the nuances out of the Fed that they are going to keep rates low for an extended period,' he said. 'It defies logic in my mind why U.S. rates are going to remain low and we expect New Zealand rates to move up in 2010. It just ain't going to happen.' \par   The simple solution to relieve upward pressure on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and rates is for the RBNZ to clearly state that domestic rates will remain low for an extended duration, Bagrie said. Whether it needs to do that before its next formal review of the OCR is a moot point. \par   RBNZ Gov. Alan Bollard made it clear at the March review that there is a limit to how low he wants the OCR to go because as it approaches zero, interest-rate cuts have less impact. As well, with New Zealand running a huge external deficit - equivalent to 8.9% of gross domestic product - the country needs high rates to attract foreign capital, he said. \par        Unorthodox Measures \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The RBNZ may have to contemplate unorthodox measures to keep wholesale rates down, Potter said.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 New Zealand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar is not an option because the RBNZ has clearly stated moves to influence the Kiwi's direction are unlikely to stick unless it is near what the bank considers an extremity of its band.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Kiwi fell from a record peak above 82 U.S. cents in March 2008 to below 48 U.S. cents this month, helping to keep New Zealand exports steady despite the global recession.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Instead, the bank might take a leaf out of the Fed's new \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  book and buy securities, Potter said. But rather than buy bonds it might become a receiver in the swaps market as swaps in New Zealand are the basis for mortgage rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The RBNZ has never \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the interest-rate swap market and such a move would likely cause a stir, as when it first \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market in June 2007 - sending the Kiwi plunging about 3 U.S. cents.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand swap rates underwent a massive slump in the last year - the 10-year rate fell from 7.8% a year ago to 4.37% early last month - before their sharp reversal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mortgage rates, which didn't match the decline in IRS rates, are now headed higher, against the wishes of the RBNZ.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ASB Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  Thursday hiked its three-year mortgage lending rate to 6.5%, its second hike in as many weeks. \f28 \uc2 \f2 ANZ National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  this week lifted its three-year rate to 6.15% from 5.99% to match similar moves by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \uc2 \f2  and ASB last week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While receiving swaps could add to the RBNZ's burgeoning balance-sheet risk, 'it would be pretty definitive and hard for the market to back up too much,' Potter said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bagrie said the idea of going down the quantitative-easing route would be 'a real stretch' because New Zealand lacks the benefit of scale, nor is it a global benchmark in the allocation of capital.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bagrie said Friday's fourth-quarter GDP data may be so poor that they act as a wake-up to markets, spurring a reversal of the alarming upward trend in interest rates and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . If markets respond, that may ease pressure on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to act quickly.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ANZ forecasts a a 1.2% fall in GDP on quarter, worse than the RBNZ's projection of a 0.8% contraction. The median forecast of 13 economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires is for a 1.0% fall - the biggest GDP decline in almost 20 years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 'I think that might give the market digestive problems later in the week,' Bagrie said. -By Simon Louisson, Dow Jones Newswires; 64-4-471-5990; simon.louisson@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Click here to go to Dow Jones NewsPlus, a web front page of today's most important business and market news, analysis and commentary: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnewsplus.com/access/al?rnd=oZauZN%2F%2B32gLvb5mD8b3IA%3D%3D"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.djnewsplus.com/access/al?rnd=oZauZN%2F%2B32gLvb5mD8b3IA%3D%3D}}}\uc2 \f2 . You can use this link on the day this article is published and the following day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -0-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Copyright (c) 2009 \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020090327e53r00231\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
