\par\par \b \uc2 NZ Dlr Taps 4-Wk High Late On Equities Surge, RBNZ Policy Cue\b0\par\par\uc2 642 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2009\par 05:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                           Late        Change \par  NZD/USD                 0.5205       +0.0071 \par  NZD/AUD                 0.7954       +0.0051 \par  NZD/JPY                 50.80        +1.44 \par  Nov 2011 Bond           3.450%       +3.0 bp \par  Dec 2017 Bond           4.760%       +1.0 bp \par  10-Year U.S. Spread     +189 bps     +7.0 bps \par  90-Day Bank Bill        3.360%       +1.0 bp \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 WELLINGTON (Dow Jones)--The New Zealand dollar rallied to a one-month peak Friday as a confluence of positive factors attracted buyers as global equities, led by Wall Street, surged on improved sentiment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Bonds eased but losses were contained by a strong auction of NZ$150 million of government paper, while swap rates continued to push higher following on from Thursday's less dovish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy stance.\par \par \uc2 The dramatic \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight by the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  to stem the rise in the Swiss franc was the main focus in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. The SNB decision also shifted attention to the Bank of Japan, which is facing similar problems with deflation and a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Talk the Bank of Japan may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  lifted the Kiwi/Yen, while the Reserve \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's comment Thursday that it wanted to maintain a yield advantage also boosted this cross rate, traders said. The Kiwi rose to a two-month high against the yen of Y51.10, and a five-week peak against the Swiss franc of CHF0.6198.\par \par \uc2 "All this positive news of rallying markets and a less dovish statement from the RBNZ has helped the Kiwi. And we have a weakening U.S. dollar, which is supporting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Daniel Brdanovic, a dealer with \uc2 HSBC\uc2  in Auckland. The New Zealand dollar surged to a high of US$0.5225 in late morning trade - the highest level since Feb. 16.\par \par \uc2 Despite data earlier Friday showing New Zealand retail sales for January falling sharply, Brdanovic said the Kiwi has a raft of factors supporting it.\par \par \uc2 A sustained rally on Wall Street may allow the Kiwi to target Fibonacchi resistance at US$0.5235, he said. Overnight, U.S. stocks got a boost from strong performance in financial stocks, which fed into broad-based gains in Asia.\par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar got a strong boost from the RBNZ on Thursday, which indicated it was nearing the end of its easing cycle and that further cuts would be small.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut the Official Cash Rate 50 basis points to an all-time low of 3.0% but said it's aggressive rate cuts will substantially slow.\par \par \uc2 RBNZ Governor Alan Bollard reiterated this message Friday in an interview with Radio New Zealand. He said that if rates are too low it will hurt the economy as depositors will be affected, which will in turn hurt some of the funding avenues.\par \par \uc2 "In addition we do rely on funding from overseas and that simply isn't going to come here if there isn't a reasonable rate for it. So we don't think that the model of interest rates going to near zero is actually appropriate for us."\par \par \uc2 In the debt market, bonds eased only slightly as the government's bond tender went solidly, with a strong bid-to-cover ratio. "we can't say precisely why the demand was so strong but it looks like a bit of offshore interest. It was quite a surprise because we didn't expect this tender to go that well based on recent ones," said one trader.\par \par \uc2 Swap rates continued to move higher on paying interest as the RBNZ's more conservative approach to easing policy rates kept receivers at bay.\par \par \uc2 -By Shri Navaratnam, Dow Jones Newswires; 64-4-471-5990; shri.navaratnam@dowjones.com [ 13-03-09 0408GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1062 20711 4171   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020090313e53d0003v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
