\par\par \b \uc2 NZ dollar off lows on Aussie  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 ; debt firm\b0\par\par\uc2 470 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 2008\par 21:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON, Nov 13 (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar <NZD=> picked itself up from a two-week low on Thursday, buoyed by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but remained under pressure amid growing risk aversion.\par \par \uc2 The kiwi had fallen more than 4 percent against the U.S. dollar to $0.5555, its lowest point since Oct. 29, in the offshore session, after U.S. Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson backed away from using a $700 billion bailout fund to clean up bank balance sheets.\par \par \uc2 But kiwi found a base in the onshore session as investors booked some profits after the yen's strong rise, and then benefited from a lift in the neighbouring Aussie dollar.\par \par \uc2 "The NZ dollar found a base in line with the Aussie following confirmation of the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Sue Trinh.\par \par \uc2 At 0400 GMT the kiwi was at $0.5611/21 compared with $0.5759/62 in late local trade on Wednesday. It ranged between $0.5545 and 0.5640 through the local session.\par \par \uc2 Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier said it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the Aussie \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an illiquid market [nSYD366201].\par \par \uc2 However, sentiment towards the kiwi was not helped by local data that showed a pessimistic view of the current and future outlook for the New Zealand economy.\par \par \uc2 Data showed retail sales volumes 0.9 percent in the three months to Sept. 30, the third consecutive quarterly decline, backing views the recession has deepened. See [nWEL347218].\par \par \uc2 The latest real estate industry survey showed house sales were flat but prices rose slightly in October [nWEL000787], while manufacturing activity fell to a record low in the Business NZ latest perfomance of manufacturing index [nWEL996].\par \par \uc2 New Zealand's economic outlook also looked gloomier after the Treasury updated its pre-election forecasts showing larger government deficits, higher debt, and lower growth. See [nSYD354756].\par \par \uc2 "It was a very weak data flow -- consistent with our view the RBNZ will cut by at least 100 basis points in December, they are behind the curve in regards to the global slowdown," said RBC's Trinh.\par \par \uc2 New Zealand debt rallied for a third session with local bills <0#NBB:> now fully pricing in a 100 basis point rate cut at the RBNZ's Dec. 4 monetary statement.\par \par \uc2 The yield on the benchmark 90 day bank bill <NZ90DBB=RNZL>, which moves inversely to prices, was 23 basis points lower.\par \par \uc2 An auction of NZ$125 million of NZ government bonds was three times covered <NZDMO04>.\par \par \uc2 Bonds held their gains of previous days, with the benchmark 10-year bond <NZ10YT=RR> steady at 5.89 percent. (Reporting by Gyles Beckford)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-NEWZEALAND-DOLLAR (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ND|ABN|D|M|E|RBN|ABX|AUF|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081115e4bc000h6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
