\par\par \b \uc2 NZ Dollar Ends Up,Takes Solid Momentum Into 08 On Soft US Dlr   \b0\par\par\uc2 641 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Dezember 2007\par 04:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                            Close        Change \par   NZD/USD                  0.7745       +0.0047 \par   NZD/AUD                  0.8805       -0.0014 \par   July 2009 Bond           7.370%       +4.5 bps \par   Dec 2017 Bond            6.385%       +3.0 bps \par   10-Year U.S. Spread      +229 bps     +13 bps \par   90-Day Bank Bill         8.890%       -5.0 bps \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 WELLINGTON (Dow Jones)--The New Zealand dollar rose to a two-week peak Monday, carrying strong momentum into 2008, as its U.S. counterpart continued to suffer from the U.S. subprime crisis that has shaken financial markets this year.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Traders say the Kiwi's immediate prospects appear favorable against a backdrop of weak U.S. economic data, amplified by a report Friday showing U.S. new home sales for November posting its biggest on-year drop in nearly 17 years.   \par \par \uc2 "The U.S. housing data continue to surprise on the downside. This credit crisis may have more to play out next year so it will be quite a difficult time for markets," said Daniel Brdanovic, a senior trader at  \uc2 HSBC\uc2  in Wellington.   \par \par \uc2 Brdanovic said the Kiwi's fortunes could go in either direction next year, adding that global uncertainty has clouded the outlook.   \par \par \uc2 The Kiwi, which rose to a two-week peak of US$0.7768 during morning trade, has advanced by around 10.3% against the U.S. dollar this year, after closing 2006 around US$0.7043. The gains have come despite the unprecedented \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in the middle of this year, aimed at capping the high-flying Kiwi.   \par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  June 11 by selling the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar for the first time since the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was floated in 1985.   \par \par \uc2 The June 11 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  sent the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar down by nearly 2% from around US$0.7630. Since then, traders say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had apparently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at least twice to kneecap the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with little success.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose to a fresh 25-year peak of US$0.8110 in July before the U.S. subprime crisis knocked it back.   \par \par \uc2 Brdanovic said that despite a slowing domestic economy, rising inflation pressures will likely keep interest rates high in New Zealand and underpin the Kiwi's yield appeal.   \par \par \uc2 Economic prospects in the U.S., which appear poor at this stage, are also set to keep buyers interested in the Kiwi, he said.   \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the Kiwi was at Y86.52 versus Y87.39 Friday.   \par \par \uc2 ANZ Bank\uc2  senior dealer Alex Sinton said the New Zealand dollar's near term outlook favors more gains. "The overriding issues are weak data in the U.S. and a weak U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and given that they favor a strong euro, the Kiwi will look to test 78 (US) cents," Sinton said.   \par \par \uc2 Thin trading conditions are likely to exaggerate some of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements, he said, adding that most traders in global markets won't be back for another two weeks.   \par \par \uc2 Government bonds finished down in an extremely subdued session, with the market closing at midday for the holiday period.   \par \par \uc2 A local trader said some receiving in swaps and selling of bonds may have impacted trading. However, "I won't read too much into today's prices...it could be a bit of end-of-year wash-up going on," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Given a shortened trading week, the trader expects a quiet period for the rest of this week. "We can't really talk about the trends in the market until next week."   \par \par \uc2 New Zealand markets will be closed Tuesday and Wednesday for the New Year public holidays.   \par \par \uc2 -By Shri Navaratnam, Dow Jones Newswires; 61-2-8235-2964; shri.navaratnam@dowjones.com [ 31-12-07 0335GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 1062 20711 4171   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020071231e3cv0001o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
