\par\par \b \uc2 NZ Dollar Down Sharply; Credit Concerns Put Bias To Downside   \b0\par\par\uc2 507 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 August 2007\par 07:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                           Close        Change \par  NZD/USD                  0.7587       -0.0089 \par  NZD/AUD                  0.8880       -0.0075 \par  July 2009 Bond           7.760%       -2.0 bp \par  Dec 2017 Bond            6.390%       -2.0 bps \par  10-Year U.S. Spread      +169 bps     +5.0 bps \par  90-Day Bank Bill         8.58%        unchanged \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 WELLINGTON (Dow Jones)--The New Zealand dollar continued to be held hostage to global credit market concerns, closing sharply lower Monday as investors cut back risky bets on renewed worries about subprime problems in the U.S.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Traders said high-yield \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Kiwi will remain under pressure this week and their fortunes tied to global equity markets.   \par \par \uc2 Michael Gordon, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Westpac Bank in Wellington, expects the New Zealand dollar to weaken during the week. "Markets have remained fixated on U.S. credit concerns, leaving the New Zealand dollar to take its direction, almost point-for-point, from global equity markets," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Wall Street's slump Friday and sharp losses in Asian markets, including New Zealand and Japan, kept investors focussed on the liquidation of carry trades. Global share markets have been responding to fears that the readily available supply of easy credit could be coming to an end as defaults in the risky U.S. subprime mortgage market make lenders more cautious about riskier lending.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bond markets largely ignored key second-quarter domestic wages data released Monday, partly as the figures came in line with market expectations.   \par \par \uc2 "There are some bigger themes out there in the market that are more important at this stage...this credit market problem doesn't want to go away and this environment is bearish for the Kiwi," said Daniel Brdanovic, a senior trader at  \uc2 HSBC\uc2  in Auckland.   \par \par \uc2 He said the risks are for the Kiwi to ease toward US$0.7530 and test US$0.7500 this week.   \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the Kiwi closed at Y89.22 from Y91.51 Friday.   \par \par \uc2 Traders say the market will watch the more important second-quarter jobs data closely, especially following the dovish undertones in the Reserve Bank of New Zealand's policy statement last month.   \par \par \uc2 The RBNZ released data showing it sold a net NZ$720 million of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars in June, when it first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to sell the Kiwi.   \par \par \uc2 The June figures don't provide complete data on the amount the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent in its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the numbers include liabilities of the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  Debt Management Office. The Kiwi hardly moved on the data.   \par \par \uc2 Government bonds rose slightly and swap rates fell, but local markets underperformed their offshore counterparts. Traders say a lack of liquidity and a holiday in New South Wales contributed to thin activity.   \par \par \uc2 Overall demand for bonds is likely to remain fairly solid due to a shortage of long-dated maturity, they said.   \par \par \uc2 -By Shri Navaratnam, Dow Jones Newswires; 64-4-471-5990; shri.navaratnam@dowjones.com [ 06-08-07 0521GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 1062 20711 4171   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070806e3860005x\par }\page {\par