\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 4-New Zealand ups rates, sees no further tightening   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kazunori Takada   \par \uc2 676 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juli 2007\par 00:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates New Zealand dollar in paragraph 6)   \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON, July 26 (Reuters) - New Zealand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised interest rates on Thursday by a quarter point to 8.25 percent to quell inflation, but indicated it would stay put for a while on signs of an easing in domestic borrowing.   \par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar <NZD=> fell from 22-year highs after Reserve Bank of New Zealand Governor Alan Bollard said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's four consecutive rate rises would be sufficient to contain price pressures.   \par \par \uc2 "The tone of the short statement was clearly more dovish than the market had been expecting. While maintaining a tightening bias, the RBNZ looks to be on hold," said Su-Lin Ong, senior economist at  \uc2 RBC Capital Markets.\par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll of 15 economists after the decision showed all saw a cash rate of 8.25 percent as the peak and most expected it to remain there at least until June next year. [nWEL235517].   \par \par \uc2 "It is clear that the RBNZ would prefer not to lift rates any further largely due to its concern over the soaring New Zealand dollar," Ong said.   \par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar <NZD=> immediately lost ground after the decision and fell as low as $0.7962, down half a cent compared with before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision, before recovering slightly. [nWEL267425]   \par \par \uc2 The yield on 90-day bank bill <NZ90DBB=RNZL> rose 5 basis points to 8.54 percent.   \par \par \uc2 Worried about inflationary pressures from the tight labour market and rising oil and food prices, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised the official cash rate to a record 8.25 percent -- the highest since it was introduced at 4.5 percent in 1999.   \par \par \uc2 "New Zealanders have been showing early signs of moderating their borrowing," Bollard said. "Provided they keep up, and the pressures on resources continue to ease, we think the four successive increases we have delivered will be sufficient to contain inflation."   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  CONCERNS   \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have based its comments on anecdotal evidence or unpublished figures, because the data so far did not suggest an easing in domestic borrowing.   \par \par \uc2 Bollard told Reuters in an interview in June that he would not relax monetary policy until he saw clear evidence of a slowdown in consumer demand and house price inflation.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised interest rates by 25 basis points in March, April and June, but is starting the get worried about the strong New Zealand dollar, or kiwi.   \par \par \uc2 Bollard reiterated on Thursday the New Zealand dollar was hurting the export sector and that its current high level was not sustainable in the medium term.   \par \par \uc2 Investors have been pouring funds into the kiwi in carry trades, where they borrow in low-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as the yen, to invest in high-yielding ones.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at least three times since June 11 to moderate the pace of the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar's rise, the first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was floated 22 years ago.   \par \par \uc2 However, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen more than 6 percent since the bank unexpectedly raised rates to 8 percent on June 7.   \par \par \uc2 Yield-hungry investors could start favouring the neighbouring Australian dollar <AUD=> on expectations the Reserve Bank of Australia will raise rates to 6.5 percent as early as August, some analysts said.   \par \par \uc2 In contrast with Bollard, Reserve Bank of Australia chief Glenn Stevens said last week he was not surprised by the strength in the Australian dollar -- a signal markets took as meaning the Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in no hurry to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the Aussie's rise to 18-year highs.   \par \par \uc2 Others cautioned it was too early to conclude the RBNZ had finished tightening.   \par \par \uc2 "We are not yet convinced that the job is done," said  \uc2 Westpac\uc2  chief economic Brendan O'Donovan. "We are still a long way from the RBNZ taking further rate hikes completely off the table."   \par \par \uc2 NEWZEALAND-ECONOMY/RATES (UPDATE 4)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|ND|AUF|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070725e37p002uu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
