\par\par \b \uc2 NZ Fin Min Brings Focus Back To RBNZ's Mandate As NZD Surges   \b0\par\par\uc2 651 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juli 2007\par 02:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON (Dow Jones)--New Zealand Finance Minister Michael Cullen appears to be causing ripples in local financial markets after telling Parliament Tuesday that the government has the capacity to suspend the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's single focus on inflation.   \par \par \uc2 Persistently high domestic inflation has forced Reserve Bank of New Zealand Governor Alan Bollard to raise interest rates in three quick moves this year, taking the Official Cash Rate to a record 8.00% - the highest policy rate in the developed world after Iceland. The consequent rush by offshore investors to buy into New Zealand dollar-denominated assets has driven the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to record highs.   \par \par \uc2 Cullen's comments on the government's Policy Targets Agreement, or PTA, with the RBNZ came after the minister labeled the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  "significantly overvalued."   \par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar reached a fresh 25-year peak of US$0.7941 Tuesday, prompting renewing debate in political circles on the negative implications for the local economy, and the RBNZ's inflation targeting regime.   \par \par \uc2 Even the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's unprecedented \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market June 11 - the first since the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was floated in March 1985 - has failed to stop the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar's surge.   \par \par \uc2 According to dealers, the bank apparently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on at least two more occasions with little success.   \par \par \uc2 Bank of New Zealand \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Danica Hampton said Wednesday's media coverage of Cullen's views may have caused a little doubt in the market about whether the RBNZ would hike interest rates again next week.   \par \par \uc2 "The press has been highlighting the Cullen comments on the PTA act...this could be causing a little bit of doubt to creep in on a hike," she said.   \par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar's modest pullback in early Wednesday trade may be partially linked to these comments, she said. The Kiwi, which fell to a low of US$0.7886 in morning trade from US$0.7916, has since recovered to US$0.7906 around 2345 GMT.   \par \par \uc2 In answer to a question in Parliament Tuesday, Cullen said the RBNZ act gives the government the power to override the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's objective of price stability.   \par \par \uc2 Cullen could instruct the RBNZ to change its objective, but that would require a new PTA to be signed. According to the Dominion Post newspaper, the minister later said he was merely pointing out that the capacity existed to alter the PTA's objectives, rather than signaling any intention to do so.   \par \par \uc2 Critics Say PTA Has Narrow Focus   \par \par \uc2 Under the existing PTA, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief is required to maintain annual inflation within a 1%-3% target band over the medium term.   \par \par \uc2 Critics charge that the bank's narrow focus on inflation has led to unnecessary interest rate increases, and sharp gains in the New Zealand dollar.   \par \par \uc2 Headline inflation in New Zealand eased to 2% on year in the second quarter, within the RBNZ's target band, but domestic inflation - which excludes import prices - remains at an uncomfortably high 4.1%.   \par \par \uc2 A slim majority of economists expect Bollard to raise interest rates by a quarter percentage point to 8.25% at next week's policy meeting.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders and economists say the New Zealand dollar could rise above US$0.8000 as long as inflation remains strong, putting pressure on the RBNZ to tighten policy.   \par \par \uc2 Westpac Bank chief economist Brendan O'Donovan said in a report that Bollard's "nightmare scenario" is that he will have to raise interest rates again next week, and watch the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  soar above US$0.8000.   \par \par \uc2 He said Westpac is taking the RBNZ at its word when it said last month that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "does not prejudge the future direction of monetary policy."   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Shri Navaratnam, Dow Jones Newswires; 64-4-471-5990; shri.navaratnam@dowjones.com \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 18-07-07 0036GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070718e37i0000n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
