\par\par \b \uc2 NZ Dollar Ends Higher, Dealers Say May Gain More; Bonds Up   \b0\par\par\uc2 445 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juni 2007\par 07:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                           Close        Change \par  NZD/USD                  0.7665       +0.0020 \par  NZD/AUD                  0.9028       -0.0002 \par  July 2009 Bond           7.315%       -1.5 bps \par  Dec 2017 Bond            6.740%       -2.0 bps \par  10-Year U.S. Spread      +161.0 bps   +1.0 bp \par  90-Day Bank Bill         8.37%        unchanged \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 WELLINGTON (Dow Jones)--The New Zealand dollar closed higher Monday after touching another new 25-year high in intraday trade, and dealers said it may rise further.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at ANZ in Wellington, Mark Elliot, said the local unit's upward momentum is intact while it remains above US$0.7650.   \par \par \uc2 "The market's probably still short (New Zealand dollars) and it has potential to trade up to US$0.7700 or US$0.7725," Elliott said, though he added this is dependent on continued U.S. dollar weakness, yield demand and this week's economic data.   \par \par \uc2 The Kiwi tapped a fresh 25-year peak of US$0.7684 during the session, buoyed by carry trade demand. Traders said it appears solidly supported above US$0.7580 as a result of this demand, coming mostly from Asia.   \par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar climbed to Y94.98 from Friday's close of Y94.72.   \par \par \uc2 The market earlier shrugged off rumors the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars late Friday in the New York session, which saw the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pull back towards US$0.7600.   \par \par \uc2 It was the second time the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is rumored to have acted to cap the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains since it confirmed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on June 11.   \par \par \uc2 The market is expected to take its cue from a raft of local economic indicators this week, including first quarter current account and gross domestic product data and reports on consumer and business confidence.   \par \par \uc2 "If anything comes out on the soft side of expectations, it could well be a catalyst for some position unwinding," said ANZ's Elliott.   \par \par \uc2 The interest rate swap was little changed and government bonds slightly better bid ahead of the data.   \par \par \uc2 "More people are expecting that, on balance, the data will prove inflationary for the RBNZ, and that should limit any significant receiving interest from emerging," said one local trader.   \par \par \uc2 "The bond market looks like it will track U.S. Treasurys, but again I don't see us pushing prices sharply either way until the markets have had a chance to look at the data," he said.   \par \par \uc2 -By Symon Ross, Dow Jones Newswires; 64-4-471-5990; symon.ross@dowjones.com   \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Roger Ng [ 25-06-07 0539GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 1062 20711 4171   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070625e36p0005f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
