\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK \highlight\uc2  WATCH: RBNZ  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Muddies Rate Outlook   \b0\par\par\uc2 783 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juni 2007\par 05:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Asian Equities Report\par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Rachel Pannett \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 WELLINGTON (Dow Jones)--The actions of New Zealand's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the past two weeks have sent a strong signal to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders about its willingness to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, but left interest-rate traders in a fog.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 By selling the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time since the New Zealand dollar was floated in 1985, on June 11 and again on Monday, the Reserve Bank of New Zealand made clear that it won't tolerate further sharp appreciation in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would normally indicate that it has an easing bias as a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  boosts exports and the overall economy.   \par \par \uc2 But the Reserve Bank of New Zealand is unlikely to want to encourage expectations of looser policy given that domestic inflation is forecast to return to the upper end of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target zone and the country's housing sector and credit growth remain overheated.   \par \par \uc2 With the RBNZ not set to meet again until July 26, investors face a long wait before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's governor, Alan Bollard, provides more clarity on what \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  means for monetary policy.   \par \par \uc2 So far, financial market pricing indicates that investors have lowered their expectations of a further rise in New Zealand's official cash rate, which the RBNZ raised to 8.0% in June.   \par \par \uc2 Financial futures prices put the chance of a rate rise in July at around 19%, down from 50% just before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market a little over week ago.   \par \par \uc2 "Clearly the RBNZ is uncomfortable with both the strength of the domestic economy and the strength of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . However, a lack of a clear and consistent view may ultimately undermine any attempts to ease pressure on either (the economy or the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 )," Macquarie analyst Hayden Atkins said.   \par \par \uc2 RBNZ Executes Another "Communications Clanger"   \par \par \uc2 Bank of New Zealand's head of market economics, Stephen Toplis, said the RBNZ's decision to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets was "another communications clanger".   \par \par \uc2 "If Alan Bollard wanted to ease monetary policy... that is certainly what he achieved," Toplis said.   \par \par \uc2 This isn't the first time Bollard's actions have confused markets.   \par \par \uc2 In March, financial markets pounced on the governor's comments that the RBNZ was assessing alternative measures to slow the housing market aside from raising the official cash rate.   \par \par \uc2 Market participants took those comments as a sign that the March hike would be the last this cycle but the RBNZ went on to lift rates at each of the next two reviews.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said last week that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "doesn't prejudge the future direction of monetary policy, which as always will remain dependent on emerging market trends".   \par \par \uc2 But the fact that the RBNZ felt comfortable \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  suggests "it has no intention to raise rates further", Toplis said, although he puts the probability of further tightening in July at 40%.   \par \par \uc2 "We still think there's a very strong probability that the domestic data will prove much more inflationary than the bank currently has built into its forecasts. As such, strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  or no, rates will need to rise," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Michael Gordon, a Wellington-based market strategist for  \uc2 Westpac Banking Corp.\uc2 , agreed that if anything, successful \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to push down the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  "increases the likelihood of another rate hike".   \par \par \uc2 With inflation threatening to return to the upper end of the RBNZ's 1% to 3% target band in the second half of calendar 2008, the bank can't afford to add to net demand, either via the domestic or export sectors, Gordon said.   \par \par \uc2 "Any boost to the export sector will have to be offset by an even greater slowdown in the domestic sector - which means more rate hikes," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Of course, any further rate hikes would make the New Zealand dollar even more attractive and offset the impact of last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's high official cash rate - currently the second highest in the developed world after Iceland - underpins the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yield appeal.   \par \par \uc2 Traders note that persistent demand from Asian speculative investors, as well as real money demand out of Japan - as people look for high-yielding homes for their summer bonuses, is preventing the New Zealand dollar from a significant break below US$0.7500.   \par \par \uc2 In trading Wednesday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  touched US$0.7601, its highest level since the RBNZ first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on June 11.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Rachel Pannett, Dow Jones Newswires; 64-4-471-5990; rachel.pannett@dowjones.com    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Iain McDonald [ 20-06-07 0329GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 1184 4253   \par \par \uc2 Dokument AER0000020070620e36k0000k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
