\par\par \b \uc2 Aussie dlr near 18-yr highs, aided by carry trades   \b0\par\par\uc2 475 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juni 2007\par 01:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY, June 20 (Reuters) - The Australian dollar consolidated just near 18-year peaks against the U.S. dollar on Wednesday, drawing support from the insatiable appetite among investors for high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 The Aussie struck fresh 16-year highs against the yen, rising as high as 104.52 yen. Carry trades showed no signs of any let up as minutes from the Bank of Japan policy meeting suggested that interest rates there were unlikely to rise in a hurry.   \par \par \uc2 "Carry trades are here to stay and the view from Japan is rates are unlikely to rise," said Robert Rennie, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at  \uc2 Westpac Banking Corp.\par \par \uc2 "For carry trades to be unwound, it would need coordinated verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from central banks globally. In an environment where is risk is very much favoured, any pullback by the Aussie is a good buying opportunity."   \par \par \uc2 In carry trades, investors borrow in the low-yielding Japanese yen to buy the higher yielding Aussie or kiwi. \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week to drive down its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the kiwi recovered, helped mainly by carry trades.   \par \par \uc2 At 4:00 p.m. (0600 GMT), the Aussie was quoted at $0.8466/71 against the U.S. dollar, up from $0.8434/39 here late on Tuesday. It had risen to a near two-week high of $0.8474 during the day, just off its 18-year peak of $0.8477 hit on June 7.   \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar slipped as investors unwound positions in dollar-denominated fixed income assets after U.S. bond yields continued to fall from five-year highs struck last week.   \par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasuries rallied on Tuesday after weak housing data reinforced a view that the housing sector would remain a drag on the U.S. economy.   \par \par \uc2 The spread between two-year Australian and U.S. government debt expanded to 145 basis points, from 136 points at the end of last week, as yields on U.S. Treasuries fell at a faster pace than their Aussie counterparts.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts said attractive Aussie rates, which were amongst the highest in the industrialised world, would continue to draw investment flows, especially from Japanese retail investors seeking higher returns.   \par \par \uc2 "Investment flows and extended gains for the Aussie against the yen have underpinned the Aussie. We are yet to see the catalyst for a break lower for the Aussie as global and domestic growth remains robust," said Tony Morriss, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ANZ.   \par \par \uc2 Although Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has kept the cash rate unchanged at 6.25 percent in the past seven months, financial markets are pricing in a rate hike by October, a move which could boost the Aussie.   \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-AUSTRALIA-DOLLAR (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|AUF|ABX|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070622e36j00282\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
