* Rising stock and commodity markets were also pushing up the Aussie.   \par \par \uc2  * Shortly after midday, the Aussie dollar traded at 103.98/104.08 yen, up from 102.90/103.00 yen on Friday. It struck a 16-year high of 104.00 yen earlier in the session.   \par \par \uc2  * It was quoted at $0.8419/23, compared with $0.8367/70 late on Friday. It traded in a $0.8400 to $0.8429 range.   \par \par \uc2  * The Aussie also nudged up against the New Zealand dollar <AUDNZD=R>, which came under pressure after another suspected bout of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBNZ).   \par \par \uc2  * The \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar fell on Monday, with traders speculating that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the second time in a week.   \par \par \uc2  * Australian bond futures clung to slim gains, having rallied with U.S. Treasuries on Friday after a benign reading on U.S. core inflation boosted debt globally.   \par \par \uc2  * However, traders noted Treasuries had come under fresh selling pressure in Tokyo, suggesting the bear run of recent weeks might not have ended as yet.   \par \par \uc2  * A dearth of economic data was also keeping bond turnover light. There are no major Australian figures due this week and only a sprinkling of housing numbers in the United Sates.   \par \par \uc2  * Three-year bond futures <YTTc1> firmed to 93.545, edging away from a seven-year trough of 94.470 hit last week. The 10-year bond contract <YTCc1> reached 93.736, above a five-year low of 93.675.      \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-AUSTRALIA-BONDS/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -NOON|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|D|RBN|AUF|ABX|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070621e36i00047\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal