ed that  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Merrill Lynch & Co.\f28 \uc2 \f2  had taken control of $400 million of loan assets underlying a troubled hedge fund at  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bear Stearns Cos. Inc.\f28 \uc2 \f2  that was heavily invested in subprime loans.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bear Stearns\f28 \uc2 \f2  shares fell 1.2 percent tot $148.26, but were off their session low, after CNBC reported that Merrill had postponed a planned auction of the seized loans. For details, see [ID:nN18390330]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Dow Jones industrial average <.DJI> was down 9.83 points, or 0.07 percent, at 13,629.65. The Standard & Poor's 500 Index <.SPX> was down 0.46 of a point, or 0.03 percent, at 1,532.45. The Nasdaq Composite Index <.IXIC> was down 0.03 of a point, or unchanged on a percentage basis, at 2,626.68.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - - - -   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LONDON - The UK's leading share index drifted 0.4 percent lower on Monday, buckling under concerns over the outlook for inflation, but as ICI surged after rejecting an approach from Dutch group  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Akzo Nobel NV\f28 \uc2 \f2  .   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bucking the trend in a sliding market, ICI soared more than 15 percent, leading the index after rejecting a 7.2 billion pounds ($14.2 billion) takeover proposal from chemicals group  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Akzo Nobel.The\f28 \uc2 \f2  maker of Dulux paints, said the 600 pence-per-share proposal undervalued the company.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The FTSE 100 <.FTSE> ended down 28.9 points, or 0.43 percent, at 6,703.5, having earlier touched its highest point since September 2000 and after a stellar rally at the end of the last week.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - - - -   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO - The Nikkei average rose 0.99 percent on Monday to end at its highest in nearly four months as  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Canon Inc.\f28 \uc2 \f2  hit an all-time high on a softer yen and property stocks gained after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor suggested no rate hikes were imminent.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Kawasaki Heavy Industries Ltd.\f28 \uc2 \f2  , a supplier to  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Boeing Co.\f28 \uc2 \f2  , gained 2.5 percent on news it is considering building a new plant, while  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nintendo Co. Ltd.\f28 \uc2 \f2  climbed 2.5 percent after  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mizuho Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  raised its target price.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After the market closed,  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mitsubishi Corp.\f28 \uc2 \f2  , Japan's biggest trading company, and Australian mining company  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Murchison Metals Ltd.\f28 \uc2 \f2  said they would spend more than A$3 billion to develop an iron ore mine in Western Australia. [ID:nTKF002622]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui said on Friday he wanted to be more convinced of the sustainability of domestic capital spending and consumption, indicating the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was in no hurry to raise interest rates.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Nikkei <.N225> advanced 178.03 points to 18,149.52, the highest close since Feb. 26. The broader TOPIX index <.TOPX> gained 0.87 percent to 1,788.39.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - - - -   \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK - The yen fell to a lifetime low against the euro on Monday and hit a fresh 4-1/2-year trough against the dollar as low Japanese interest rates continued to weigh on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the dollar edged lower against the euro and sterling, tracking a slide in U.S. bond yields, undermining the appeal of dollar-denominated assets.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar hit a fresh 4-1/2-year high above 123.70 yen <JPY=> for the fourth straight session. Meanwhile the euro surged to a fresh record high of 165.82 yen <EURJPY> on electronic trading platform EBS.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar has not matched its advance against the yen with similar gains versus the euro, which rose as high as $1.3418 <EUR=> in tandem with falling U.S. bond yields, before easing to $1.3408 -- still up almost 0.2 percent from Friday.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Elsewhere, speculation that the Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 New Zealand intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Monday to weaken the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 New Zealand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar did little to sap the strength of the highest-yielder among G10 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Though an RBNZ spokeswoman declined to comment, the kiwi briefly dipped to a session low of US$0.7515 but quickly recovered. It last traded down 0.1 percent at US$0.7532 <NZD=>.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - - - -   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TREASURIES   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK - U.S. government debt prices edged up on Monday after signs of further deterioration in the housing market underscored weakness in a key area of the U.S. economy.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With benchmark yields well off last week's five-year highs above 5.30 percent, a move stoked by investors' worries about global economic growth and central banks' raising interest rates, higher yields have attracted more buyers, helping Treasuries find a foothold, analysts said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The weak housing market report might faintly revive hopes among bond investors for a Federal Reserve interest rate cut in the future, although few expect any easing of monetary policy before next year.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The benchmark U.S. Treasury 10-year note <US10YT=RR> rose 3/32 in price for a yield of 5.15 percent, compared with 5.17 percent late on Friday. Last week, 10-year yields reached a five-year high of 5.33 percent. Bond prices and yields move inversely.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 30-year bond <US30YT=RR> traded unchanged in price for a yield of 5.26 percent, compared with 5.26 percent late on Friday. Two-year notes <US2YT=RR> traded unchanged in price for a yield of 5.02 percent.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - - - -   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 COMMODITIES   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - - - -   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GOLD   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK - Gold for August delivery <GCQ7> slipped briefly into negative regions, then pulled up to trade with small gains in midday trade. Profit takers decided to take advantage of earlier 10-day highs, launched with a surge in crude oil prices to levels above $72 a barrel. But a weak dollar and strong technical support provided a solid underpinning at the lows. August gold was up $0.90 at $659.60 an ounce, in a higher trading band between $658.0 and $662.90 an ounce, a high last seen on June 8. COMEX estimated noon EDT volume at 30,510 lots. Spot gold <XAU=> rises to $656.20/7.70 an ounce, up from $654.50/6.0 an ounce in Friday's after hours trade. The London afternoon gold fix was $656.0 an ounce.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - - - -   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BASE METALS   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LONDON - Lead prices hit new record highs on Monday as the market fretted about shipments from China, while copper touched a five-week high on supply woes.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Lead for delivery in three months on the London Metal Exchange <MPB3> touched an all-time high of $2,430 a tonne in early European trade, before easing to $2,390 by the close, up against its last quote of $2,365/2,370 on Friday.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Lead, which is mainly used in battery consumption, has gained 43 percent, or $710, since the start of this year.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Three-month copper <MCU3> on LME hit $7,695 a tonne, its highest since May 16, underpinned by a string of looming strikes and low inventories. It was last at $7,540, up $40 or 0.5 percent from Friday's close.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nickel <MNI3>, which has lost more than $10,000 or 25 percent since its all-time high of $51,800 in early May, was $1,800 down at $40,400, while zinc <MZN3> was $60 softer at $3,685 and aluminium <MAL3> was at $2,705 against Friday's $2,750.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - - - -   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 OIL   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK - U.S. crude oil futures rose on Monday, bouncing off early lows to post nine-month highs and end above $69 on fresh concerns about interrupted supply or the threat of interruption in Nigeria and an oil workers' strike threat in Brazil.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the New York Mercantile Exchange, July crude <CLN7> rose $1.09, or 1.6 percent, to settle at $69.09 a barrel, trading from $67.40 to $69.15. It was the highest settlement since $69.19 on Sept. 1, 2006.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In London, August Brent <LCOQ7> last traded up 70 cents, or 0.98 percent, at $72.17 a barrel, trading from $70.72 to $72.25, a 10-month high.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - - - -   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-NEWZEALAND-GLOBAL|LANGEN|Table|ND|AUF|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070618e36i001s4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
