\par\par \b \uc2 ASIA DAILY  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  OUTLOOK - Majors   \b0\par\par\uc2 1,004 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 2007\par 01:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Following are expected trading ranges and outlooks for nine major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs in Asia today:   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par           Immediate Range  Larger Range \par  \par USD/JPY   122.89-124.00    122.10-125.70 \par EUR/USD   1.3304-1.3410    1.3262-1.3554 \par AUD/USD   0.8331-0.8445    0.8289-0.8476 \par NZD/USD   0.7493-0.7553    0.7451-0.7638 \par GBP/USD   1.9686-1.9783    1.9621-1.9968 \par USD/CHF   1.2372-1.2470    1.2332-1.2572 \par USD/CAD   1.0602-1.0739    1.0547-1.0785 \par EUR/JPY   163.56-166.00    163.07-167.00 \par EUR/GBP   0.6750-0.6787    0.6728-0.6811 \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 (Ranges are calculated using recent high and lows, information on the placement of option strikes, and technical analysis - Fibonacci levels, trendlines and moving averages.)   \par \par \uc2 USD/JPY -- to rise after it Friday posted new four-and-a-half-year highs as carry trades return to fashion after BOJ left rates unchanged, in line with expectations. Also, unanimity of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's vote to keep rates on hold surprised some, with lack of any dissenting votes viewed as dovish. Moreover, BOJ Gov. Fukui signaled he wants to study more economic data before raising interest rates, suggesting bank isn't ready to tighten rates next month as some had expected. Data focus on US Jun NAHB Housing Index, Japan June Economic Report today, US May housing starts, Japan 2Q business outlook survey tomorrow, US June Philadelphia Fed Business Index Thursday. USD/JPY faces immediate resistance at psychological 124.00, followed by Dec 2002 high of 125.70. Initial support at 122.89 (Friday low), followed by support on uptrend line from June 8 (now at 122.10).   \par \par \uc2 EUR/USD -- to rise after tame US inflation data Friday showed US Fed won't need to resort to higher rates for price stability, and after ECB board member Axel Weber made it clear that he regards euro-zone interest rates as "far from being restrictive", boosting expectations that ECB will need to hike interest rates more than once to keep a lid on inflationary pressures. Eurodollar futures September 2007 through March 2009 Eurodollar contracts at Friday's close priced in very strong probability that Fed would keep funds rate at 5.25%. Just minutes before CPI data Dec 07 priced in about 10% chance for 25bp hike to 5.5% in 4Q, having priced in no rate hike expectations Thursday. With inflation looking better, Fed now likely to focus more on weak housing sector, say some, with this week's housing numbers key. Data focus this week on Germany June ZEW indicator of economic sentiment Tuesday, euro-zone June PMI Thursday, ECB President Trichet at SNB's 100-year celebration Friday. EUR/USD has immediate resistance at 1.3410 (late May lows), followed by stronger resistance at 1.3554 (June 5 high). Initial support at 1.3304 (Friday low), followed by stronger support at 1.3262 (Wednesday low).   \par \par \uc2 AUD/USD -- has upward bias on strengthening commodity prices, growing appetite for carry trades and despite Australia's RBA Governor Glenn Stevens last week playing down need for immediate further tightening. Focus this week on Aussie Westpac-Melbourne Institute indexes of economic activity Wednesday, Aussie June RBA bulletin Thursday. AUD/USD faces immediate resistance at 0.8445 (Tuesday high), then at 0.8476 (June 7 high). Support at 0.8331 (Thursday low), then 0.8289 (55-day moving average).   \par \par \uc2 NZD/USD -- to consolidate with modest upward bias but capped by likely Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier Monday, following \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  a week ago for first time since NZD floated in 1985. Market participants say RBNZ sales pushed NZD down to 0.7500 from around 0.7550. Also capping pair is weaker-than-expected NZ April retail sales data issued last week, Friday's data showed NZ housing market may be starting to cool. NZD/USD faces resistance at 0.7553 (Friday high), followed by 0.7638 (June 8 high). Support at 0.7493 (Friday low), then 0.7451 (Wednesday low).   \par \par \uc2 GBP/USD -- has upward bias on expectations of further interest-rate hike after hawkish comments from BOE Gov. King recently. Data focus this week on UK May producer prices Tuesday, UK June BoE Monetary Policy Committee minutes Wednesday. Cable faces initial resistance at 1.9783 (Tuesday high), next at 1.9968 (June 5 high). Immediate support at 1.9686 (Friday low), next at 1.9621 (June 8 low).   \par \par \uc2 USD/CHF -- has upward bias on resumption of carry trades, despite SNB rate hike Thursday, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's hawkish statement raising its growth, inflation projections for this year. USD/CHF faces initial resistance at 1.2470 (last week highs), followed by stronger at 1.2572 (January high). Initial support at 1.2372 (Tuesday low), stronger support at 1.2332 (May 21 high).   \par \par \uc2 USD/CAD -- has soft bias as persistently high Canada core inflation could prompt BOC to raise interest rates in July. Canada data focus on international securities transactions for April due Monday, followed by May CPI on Tuesday, April wholesale trade data and leading indicators for May on Wednesday, and April retail sales on Thursday. USD/CAD has initial support at 1.0602 (Friday low), stronger support at 1.0547 (month's low). Initial resistance at 1.0739 (Wednesday high), followed by 1.0785 (38.2% retracement of fall from May).   \par \par \uc2 EUR/JPY -- to rise after it last week rebounded from strong support at 161.04 (May 11 low), this morning's rise to new all-time high of 165.27. Psychological resistance at 166.00, followed by 167.00. Initial support at 163.56 (Friday low), next at 163.07 (Thursday low).   \par \par \uc2 EUR/GBP -- has positive bias. Initial resistance at 0.6787 (Tuesday high), next at 0.6811 (May 31 high). Initial support at 0.6750 (Friday low), next at 0.6728 (Wednesday low).   \par \par \uc2 -By Manish Jaradi, Dow Jones CommentaryPlus; 65-6415-4034.   \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Iain McDonald [ 17-06-07 2342GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 1075 4033   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070617e36h000gb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
